window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH["two_noble_kinsmen"] = {"id":"two_noble_kinsmen","title":"The Two Noble Kinsmen","kind":"play","source":"Project Gutenberg #1506","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506","sourceSnapshot":"sources/gutenberg-1506.html","licenseUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/policy/license.html","attributionNote":"William Shakespeare and John Fletcher","sections":[{"id":"dramatis-personae","label":"Dramatis Personæ","act":0,"blocks":[{"kind":"text","text":"PROLOGUE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:1"},{"kind":"text","text":"ARCITE, the two noble kinsmen, cousins,\nPALAMON, nephews of Creon, King of Thebes","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:2"},{"kind":"text","text":"THESEUS, Duke of Athens\nHIPPOLYTA, Queen of the Amazons, later Duchess of Athens\nEMILIA, Sister of Hippolyta\nPIRITHOUS, friend to Theseus","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:3"},{"kind":"text","text":"Three QUEENS, widows of the kings killed in laying siege to Thebes","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:4"},{"kind":"text","text":"The JAILER of Theseus’s prison\nHis DAUGHTER, in love with Palamon\nHis BROTHER,\nThe WOOER of the Jailer’s daughter\nTwo FRIENDS of the Jailer,\nA DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:5"},{"kind":"text","text":"ARTESIUS, an Athenian soldier\nVALERIUS, a Theban\nWOMAN, attending on Emilia\nAn Athenian GENTLEMAN\nSix KNIGHTS, three accompanying Arcite, three Palamon","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:6"},{"kind":"text","text":"Six COUNTRYMEN, one dressed as a Bavian or baboon\nGerald, a SCHOOLMASTER\nNEL, a countrywoman\nA TABORER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:7"},{"kind":"text","text":"A singing BOY\nA HERALD\nA MESSENGER\nA SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:8"},{"kind":"text","text":"EPILOGUE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:9"},{"kind":"text","text":"Hymen (god of weddings), lords, soldiers, four countrywomen (Fritz, Maudlin,\nLuce, and Barbary), nymphs, attendants, maids, executioner, guard","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:10"},{"kind":"text","text":"SCENE: Athens and the Neighbourhood, except in part of the first Act,\nwhere it is Thebes and the Neighbourhood","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:dramatis-personae:11"}]},{"id":"prologue","label":"PROLOGUE","act":0,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter Prologue.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:prologue:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"New plays and maidenheads are near akin:\nMuch followed both, for both much money gi’en,\nIf they stand sound and well. And a good play,\nWhose modest scenes blush on his marriage day\nAnd shake to lose his honour, is like her\nThat after holy tie and first night’s stir\nYet still is Modesty, and still retains\nMore of the maid, to sight, than husband’s pains.\nWe pray our play may be so, for I am sure\nIt has a noble breeder and a pure,\nA learned, and a poet never went\nMore famous yet ’twixt Po and silver Trent.\nChaucer, of all admired, the story gives;\nThere, constant to eternity, it lives.\nIf we let fall the nobleness of this,\nAnd the first sound this child hear be a hiss,\nHow will it shake the bones of that good man\nAnd make him cry from underground, “O, fan\nFrom me the witless chaff of such a writer\nThat blasts my bays and my famed works makes lighter\nThan Robin Hood!” This is the fear we bring;\nFor, to say truth, it were an endless thing\nAnd too ambitious, to aspire to him,\nWeak as we are, and, almost breathless, swim\nIn this deep water. Do but you hold out\nYour helping hands, and we shall tack about\nAnd something do to save us. You shall hear\nScenes, though below his art, may yet appear\nWorth two hours’ travel. To his bones sweet sleep;\nContent to you. If this play do not keep\nA little dull time from us, we perceive\nOur losses fall so thick, we must needs leave.","speaker":"PROLOGUE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:prologue:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:prologue:3"}],"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"1.1","label":"Act 1 · SCENE I. Athens. Before a temple","act":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Hymen with a torch burning; a Boy, in a white robe before singing, and strewing\nflowers. After Hymen, a Nymph encompassed in her\ntresses, bearing a wheaten garland; then Theseus\nbetween two other Nymphs with wheaten chaplets on their heads. Then Hippolyta, the bride, led by Pirithous, and another holding a garland over her head,\nher tresses likewise hanging. After her, Emilia,\nholding up her train. Then Artesius and\nAttendants.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:1"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Music.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:2"},{"kind":"text","text":"The Song","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:3"},{"kind":"text","text":"Roses, their sharp spines being gone,\nNot royal in their smells alone,\nBut in their hue;\nMaiden pinks of odour faint,\nDaisies smell-less, yet most quaint,\nAnd sweet thyme true;","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:4"},{"kind":"text","text":"Primrose, first-born child of Ver,\nMerry springtime’s harbinger,\nWith harebells dim,\nOxlips in their cradles growing,\nMarigolds on deathbeds blowing,\nLark’s-heels trim;","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Strews flowers.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:6"},{"kind":"text","text":"All dear Nature’s children sweet\nLie ’fore bride and bridegroom’s feet,\nBlessing their sense.\nNot an angel of the air,\nBird melodious or bird fair,\nIs absent hence.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:7"},{"kind":"text","text":"The crow, the sland’rous cuckoo, nor\nThe boding raven, nor chough hoar,\nNor chatt’ring ’pie,\nMay on our bride-house perch or sing,\nOr with them any discord bring,\nBut from it fly.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter three Queens in black, with veils stained,\nwith imperial crowns. The first Queen falls down at the foot of Theseus; the second falls down at the foot of Hippolyta; the third before\nEmilia.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For pity’s sake and true gentility’s,\nHear and respect me.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For your mother’s sake,\nAnd as you wish your womb may thrive with fair ones,\nHear and respect me.","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, for the love of him whom Jove hath marked\nThe honour of your bed, and for the sake\nOf clear virginity, be advocate\nFor us and our distresses. This good deed\nShall raze you out o’ th’ book of trespasses\nAll you are set down there.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sad lady, rise.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand up.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No knees to me.\nWhat woman I may stead that is distressed,\nDoes bind me to her.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What’s your request? Deliver you for all.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We are three queens whose sovereigns fell before\nThe wrath of cruel Creon, who endure\nThe beaks of ravens, talons of the kites,\nAnd pecks of crows, in the foul fields of Thebes.\nHe will not suffer us to burn their bones,\nTo urn their ashes, nor to take th’ offence\nOf mortal loathsomeness from the blest eye\nOf holy Phœbus, but infects the winds\nWith stench of our slain lords. O, pity, Duke!\nThou purger of the earth, draw thy feared sword\nThat does good turns to th’ world; give us the bones\nOf our dead kings, that we may chapel them;\nAnd of thy boundless goodness take some note\nThat for our crowned heads we have no roof\nSave this, which is the lion’s and the bear’s,\nAnd vault to everything.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you, kneel not.\nI was transported with your speech and suffered\nYour knees to wrong themselves. I have heard the fortunes\nOf your dead lords, which gives me such lamenting\nAs wakes my vengeance and revenge for ’em.\nKing Capaneus was your lord. The day\nThat he should marry you, at such a season\nAs now it is with me, I met your groom\nBy Mars’s altar. You were that time fair!\nNot Juno’s mantle fairer than your tresses,\nNor in more bounty spread her. Your wheaten wreath\nWas then nor threshed nor blasted. Fortune at you\nDimpled her cheek with smiles. Hercules, our kinsman,\nThen weaker than your eyes, laid by his club;\nHe tumbled down upon his Nemean hide\nAnd swore his sinews thawed. O grief and time,\nFearful consumers, you will all devour!","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I hope some god,\nSome god hath put his mercy in your manhood,\nWhereto he’ll infuse power, and press you forth\nOur undertaker.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, no knees, none, widow!\nUnto the helmeted Bellona use them,\nAnd pray for me, your soldier.\nTroubled I am.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Turns away.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Honoured Hippolyta,\nMost dreaded Amazonian, that hast slain\nThe scythe-tusked boar; that with thy arm, as strong\nAs it is white, wast near to make the male\nTo thy sex captive, but that this thy lord,\nBorn to uphold creation in that honour\nFirst nature styled it in, shrunk thee into\nThe bound thou wast o’erflowing, at once subduing\nThy force and thy affection; soldieress\nThat equally canst poise sternness with pity,\nWhom now I know hast much more power on him\nThan ever he had on thee, who ow’st his strength\nAnd his love too, who is a servant for\nThe tenor of thy speech, dear glass of ladies,\nBid him that we, whom flaming war doth scorch,\nUnder the shadow of his sword may cool us;\nRequire him he advance it o’er our heads;\nSpeak ’t in a woman’s key, like such a woman\nAs any of us three; weep ere you fail.\nLend us a knee;\nBut touch the ground for us no longer time\nThan a dove’s motion when the head’s plucked off.\nTell him if he i’ th’ blood-sized field lay swollen,\nShowing the sun his teeth, grinning at the moon,\nWhat you would do.","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor lady, say no more.\nI had as lief trace this good action with you\nAs that whereto I am going, and never yet\nWent I so willing way. My lord is taken\nHeart-deep with your distress. Let him consider;\nI’ll speak anon.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my petition was\nSet down in ice, which by hot grief uncandied\nMelts into drops; so sorrow, wanting form,\nIs pressed with deeper matter.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, stand up;\nYour grief is written in your cheek.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, woe!\nYou cannot read it there. There through my tears,\nLike wrinkled pebbles in a glassy stream,\nYou may behold ’em. Lady, lady, alack!\nHe that will all the treasure know o’ th’ earth\nMust know the center too; he that will fish\nFor my least minnow, let him lead his line\nTo catch one at my heart. O, pardon me!\nExtremity, that sharpens sundry wits,\nMakes me a fool.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you say nothing, pray you.\nWho cannot feel nor see the rain, being in ’t,\nKnows neither wet nor dry. If that you were\nThe ground-piece of some painter, I would buy you\nT’ instruct me ’gainst a capital grief, indeed\nSuch heart-pierced demonstration. But, alas,\nBeing a natural sister of our sex,\nYour sorrow beats so ardently upon me\nThat it shall make a counter-reflect ’gainst\nMy brother’s heart and warm it to some pity,\nThough it were made of stone. Pray have good comfort.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forward to th’ temple! Leave not out a jot\nO’ th’ sacred ceremony.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, this celebration\nWill longer last and be more costly than\nYour suppliants’ war! Remember that your fame\nKnolls in the ear o’ th’ world; what you do quickly\nIs not done rashly; your first thought is more\nThan others’ laboured meditance, your premeditating\nMore than their actions. But, O Jove, your actions,\nSoon as they move, as ospreys do the fish,\nSubdue before they touch. Think, dear Duke, think\nWhat beds our slain kings have!","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What griefs our beds,\nThat our dear lords have none!","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"None fit for th’ dead.\nThose that with cords, knives, drams, precipitance,\nWeary of this world’s light, have to themselves\nBeen death’s most horrid agents, human grace\nAffords them dust and shadow.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But our lords\nLie blist’ring ’fore the visitating sun,\nAnd were good kings when living.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is true, and I will give you comfort\nTo give your dead lords graves;\nThe which to do must make some work with Creon.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And that work presents itself to th’ doing.\nNow ’twill take form; the heats are gone tomorrow.\nThen, bootless toil must recompense itself\nWith its own sweat. Now he’s secure,\nNot dreams we stand before your puissance,\nRinsing our holy begging in our eyes\nTo make petition clear.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now you may take him, drunk with his victory.","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And his army full of bread and sloth.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Artesius, that best knowest\nHow to draw out fit to this enterprise\nThe prim’st for this proceeding, and the number\nTo carry such a business: forth and levy\nOur worthiest instruments, whilst we dispatch\nThis grand act of our life, this daring deed\nOf fate in wedlock.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dowagers, take hands.\nLet us be widows to our woes; delay\nCommends us to a famishing hope.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell!","speaker":"ALL THE QUEENS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We come unseasonably; but when could grief\nCull forth, as unpanged judgement can, fitt’st time\nFor best solicitation?","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, good ladies,\nThis is a service, whereto I am going,\nGreater than any war; it more imports me\nThan all the actions that I have foregone,\nOr futurely can cope.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The more proclaiming\nOur suit shall be neglected when her arms,\nAble to lock Jove from a synod, shall\nBy warranting moonlight corselet thee. O, when\nHer twinning cherries shall their sweetness fall\nUpon thy tasteful lips, what wilt thou think\nOf rotten kings or blubbered queens? What care\nFor what thou feel’st not, what thou feel’st being able\nTo make Mars spurn his drum? O, if thou couch\nBut one night with her, every hour in ’t will\nTake hostage of thee for a hundred, and\nThou shalt remember nothing more than what\nThat banquet bids thee to.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though much unlike\nYou should be so transported, as much sorry\nI should be such a suitor, yet I think,\nDid I not, by th’ abstaining of my joy,\nWhich breeds a deeper longing, cure their surfeit\nThat craves a present med’cine, I should pluck\nAll ladies’ scandal on me. Therefore, sir,","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[She kneels.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As I shall here make trial of my prayers,\nEither presuming them to have some force,\nOr sentencing for aye their vigor dumb,\nProrogue this business we are going about, and hang\nYour shield afore your heart, about that neck\nWhich is my fee, and which I freely lend\nTo do these poor queens service.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To Emilia.] O, help now!\nOur cause cries for your knee.","speaker":"ALL QUEENS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To Theseus, kneeling.] If you grant not\nMy sister her petition in that force,\nWith that celerity and nature, which\nShe makes it in, from henceforth I’ll not dare\nTo ask you anything, nor be so hardy\nEver to take a husband.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray stand up.\nI am entreating of myself to do","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They rise.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That which you kneel to have me.—Pirithous,\nLead on the bride; get you and pray the gods\nFor success and return; omit not anything\nIn the pretended celebration.—Queens,\nFollow your soldier. [To Artesius.] As before, hence you,\nAnd at the banks of Aulis meet us with\nThe forces you can raise, where we shall find\nThe moiety of a number for a business\nMore bigger looked.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Artesius.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To Hippolyta.] Since that our theme is haste,\nI stamp this kiss upon thy currant lip;\nSweet, keep it as my token. Set you forward,\nFor I will see you gone.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:52"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[The wedding procession moves towards the temple.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, my beauteous sister.—Pirithous,\nKeep the feast full; bate not an hour on ’t.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir,\nI’ll follow you at heels. The feast’s solemnity\nShall want till your return.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousin, I charge you,\nBudge not from Athens. We shall be returning\nEre you can end this feast, of which I pray you\nMake no abatement. Once more, farewell all.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:56"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt all but Theseus and the\nQueens.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus dost thou still make good the tongue o’ th’ world.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And earn’st a deity equal with Mars.","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If not above him, for\nThou, being but mortal, mak’st affections bend\nTo godlike honours; they themselves, some say,\nGroan under such a mast’ry.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As we are men,\nThus should we do; being sensually subdued,\nWe lose our human title. Good cheer, ladies.\nNow turn we towards your comforts.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:61"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.1:62"}],"scene":1},{"id":"1.2","label":"Act 1 · SCENE II. Thebes. The Court of the Palace","act":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and\nArcite.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dear Palamon, dearer in love than blood\nAnd our prime cousin, yet unhardened in\nThe crimes of nature, let us leave the city\nThebes, and the temptings in ’t, before we further\nSully our gloss of youth\nAnd here to keep in abstinence we shame\nAs in incontinence; for not to swim\nI’ th’ aid o’ th’ current, were almost to sink,\nAt least to frustrate striving; and to follow\nThe common stream, ’twould bring us to an eddy\nWhere we should turn or drown; if labour through,\nOur gain but life and weakness.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your advice\nIs cried up with example. What strange ruins,\nSince first we went to school, may we perceive\nWalking in Thebes! Scars and bare weeds\nThe gain o’ th’ martialist, who did propound\nTo his bold ends honour and golden ingots,\nWhich, though he won, he had not, and now flirted\nBy peace for whom he fought! Who then shall offer\nTo Mars’s so-scorned altar? I do bleed\nWhen such I meet, and wish great Juno would\nResume her ancient fit of jealousy\nTo get the soldier work, that peace might purge\nFor her repletion, and retain anew\nHer charitable heart, now hard and harsher\nThan strife or war could be.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you not out?\nMeet you no ruin but the soldier in\nThe cranks and turns of Thebes? You did begin\nAs if you met decays of many kinds.\nPerceive you none that do arouse your pity\nBut th’ unconsidered soldier?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, I pity\nDecays where’er I find them, but such most\nThat, sweating in an honourable toil,\nAre paid with ice to cool ’em.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis not this\nI did begin to speak of. This is virtue\nOf no respect in Thebes. I spake of Thebes,\nHow dangerous, if we will keep our honours,\nIt is for our residing, where every evil\nHath a good colour; where every seeming good’s\nA certain evil; where not to be e’en jump\nAs they are here were to be strangers, and,\nSuch things to be, mere monsters.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis in our power—\nUnless we fear that apes can tutor ’s—to\nBe masters of our manners. What need I\nAffect another’s gait, which is not catching\nWhere there is faith? Or to be fond upon\nAnother’s way of speech, when by mine own\nI may be reasonably conceived, saved too,\nSpeaking it truly? Why am I bound\nBy any generous bond to follow him\nFollows his tailor, haply so long until\nThe followed make pursuit? Or let me know\nWhy mine own barber is unblessed, with him\nMy poor chin too, for ’tis not scissored just\nTo such a favourite’s glass? What canon is there\nThat does command my rapier from my hip\nTo dangle ’t in my hand, or to go tiptoe\nBefore the street be foul? Either I am\nThe fore-horse in the team, or I am none\nThat draw i’ th’ sequent trace. These poor slight sores\nNeed not a plantain; that which rips my bosom\nAlmost to th’ heart’s—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our uncle Creon.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He.\nA most unbounded tyrant, whose successes\nMakes heaven unfeared and villainy assured\nBeyond its power there’s nothing; almost puts\nFaith in a fever, and deifies alone\nVoluble chance; who only attributes\nThe faculties of other instruments\nTo his own nerves and act; commands men service,\nAnd what they win in ’t, boot and glory; one\nThat fears not to do harm; good, dares not. Let\nThe blood of mine that’s sib to him be sucked\nFrom me with leeches; let them break and fall\nOff me with that corruption.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Clear-spirited cousin,\nLet’s leave his court, that we may nothing share\nOf his loud infamy; for our milk\nWill relish of the pasture, and we must\nBe vile or disobedient; not his kinsmen\nIn blood unless in quality.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing truer.\nI think the echoes of his shames have deafed\nThe ears of heavenly justice. Widows’ cries\nDescend again into their throats and have not\nDue audience of the gods.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Valerius.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Valerius!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The King calls for you; yet be leaden-footed\nTill his great rage be off him. Phœbus, when\nHe broke his whipstock and exclaimed against\nThe horses of the sun, but whispered to\nThe loudness of his fury.","speaker":"VALERIUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Small winds shake him.\nBut what’s the matter?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Theseus, who where he threats appalls, hath sent\nDeadly defiance to him and pronounces\nRuin to Thebes, who is at hand to seal\nThe promise of his wrath.","speaker":"VALERIUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him approach.\nBut that we fear the gods in him, he brings not\nA jot of terror to us. Yet what man\nThirds his own worth—the case is each of ours—\nWhen that his action’s dregged with mind assured\n’Tis bad he goes about?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Leave that unreasoned.\nOur services stand now for Thebes, not Creon.\nYet to be neutral to him were dishonour,\nRebellious to oppose; therefore we must\nWith him stand to the mercy of our fate,\nWho hath bounded our last minute.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So we must.\n[To Valerius.] Is ’t said this war’s afoot? Or, it shall be,\nOn fail of some condition?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis in motion;\nThe intelligence of state came in the instant\nWith the defier.","speaker":"VALERIUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let’s to the King; who, were he\nA quarter carrier of that honour which\nHis enemy come in, the blood we venture\nShould be as for our health, which were not spent,\nRather laid out for purchase. But alas,\nOur hands advanced before our hearts, what will\nThe fall o’ th’ stroke do damage?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let th’ event,\nThat never-erring arbitrator, tell us\nWhen we know all ourselves; and let us follow\nThe becking of our chance.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.2:23"}],"scene":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"1.3","label":"Act 1 · SCENE III. Before the gates of Athens","act":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Pirithous, Hippolyta and\nEmilia.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No further.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, farewell. Repeat my wishes\nTo our great lord, of whose success I dare not\nMake any timorous question; yet I wish him\nExcess and overflow of power, an ’t might be,\nTo dure ill-dealing fortune. Speed to him!\nStore never hurts good governors.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though I know\nHis ocean needs not my poor drops, yet they\nMust yield their tribute there. My precious maid,\nThose best affections that the heavens infuse\nIn their best-tempered pieces keep enthroned\nIn your dear heart!","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thanks, sir. Remember me\nTo our all-royal brother, for whose speed\nThe great Bellona I’ll solicit; and\nSince in our terrene state petitions are not\nWithout gifts understood, I’ll offer to her\nWhat I shall be advised she likes. Our hearts\nAre in his army, in his tent.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In ’s bosom.\nWe have been soldiers, and we cannot weep\nWhen our friends don their helms, or put to sea,\nOr tell of babes broached on the lance, or women\nThat have sod their infants in—and after eat them—\nThe brine they wept at killing ’em. Then if\nYou stay to see of us such spinsters, we\nShould hold you here for ever.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace be to you\nAs I pursue this war, which shall be then\nBeyond further requiring.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Pirithous.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How his longing\nFollows his friend! Since his depart, his sports,\nThough craving seriousness and skill, passed slightly\nHis careless execution, where nor gain\nMade him regard, or loss consider, but\nPlaying one business in his hand, another\nDirecting in his head, his mind nurse equal\nTo these so differing twins. Have you observed him\nSince our great lord departed?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With much labour,\nAnd I did love him for ’t. They two have cabined\nIn many as dangerous as poor a corner,\nPeril and want contending; they have skiffed\nTorrents whose roaring tyranny and power\nI’ th’ least of these was dreadful; and they have\nFought out together where Death’s self was lodged;\nYet fate hath brought them off. Their knot of love,\nTied, weaved, entangled, with so true, so long,\nAnd with a finger of so deep a cunning,\nMay be outworn, never undone. I think\nTheseus cannot be umpire to himself,\nCleaving his conscience into twain and doing\nEach side like justice, which he loves best.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Doubtless\nThere is a best, and reason has no manners\nTo say it is not you. I was acquainted\nOnce with a time when I enjoyed a playfellow;\nYou were at wars when she the grave enriched,\nWho made too proud the bed, took leave o’ th’ moon\nWhich then looked pale at parting, when our count\nWas each eleven.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Twas Flavina.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.\nYou talk of Pirithous’ and Theseus’ love.\nTheirs has more ground, is more maturely seasoned,\nMore buckled with strong judgement, and their needs\nThe one of th’ other may be said to water\nTheir intertangled roots of love; but I,\nAnd she I sigh and spoke of, were things innocent,\nLoved for we did, and like the elements\nThat know not what nor why, yet do effect\nRare issues by their operance, our souls\nDid so to one another. What she liked\nWas then of me approved, what not, condemned,\nNo more arraignment. The flower that I would pluck\nAnd put between my breasts, O, then but beginning\nTo swell about the blossom—she would long\nTill she had such another, and commit it\nTo the like innocent cradle, where, phœnix-like,\nThey died in perfume. On my head no toy\nBut was her pattern; her affections—pretty,\nThough haply her careless wear—I followed\nFor my most serious decking; had mine ear\nStol’n some new air, or at adventure hummed one\nFrom musical coinage, why, it was a note\nWhereon her spirits would sojourn—rather, dwell on,\nAnd sing it in her slumbers. This rehearsal,\nWhich fury-innocent wots well, comes in\nLike old importment’s bastard—has this end,\nThat the true love ’tween maid and maid may be\nMore than in sex individual.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You’re out of breath;\nAnd this high-speeded pace is but to say\nThat you shall never, like the maid Flavina,\nLove any that’s called man.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sure I shall not.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, alack, weak sister,\nI must no more believe thee in this point—\nThough in ’t I know thou dost believe thyself—\nThan I will trust a sickly appetite,\nThat loathes even as it longs. But sure, my sister,\nIf I were ripe for your persuasion, you\nHave said enough to shake me from the arm\nOf the all-noble Theseus; for whose fortunes\nI will now in and kneel, with great assurance\nThat we, more than his Pirithous, possess\nThe high throne in his heart.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am not\nAgainst your faith, yet I continue mine.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.3:18"}],"scene":3,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"1.4","label":"Act 1 · SCENE IV. A field before Thebes.","act":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Cornets. A battle struck within; then a retreat. Flourish. Then enter, Theseus, as victor, with a Herald, other Lords, and Soldiers. The three Queens meet him and fall on their faces before him.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To thee no star be dark!","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Both heaven and earth\nFriend thee for ever!","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All the good that may\nBe wished upon thy head, I cry “Amen” to ’t!","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Th’ impartial gods, who from the mounted heavens\nView us their mortal herd, behold who err\nAnd, in their time, chastise. Go and find out\nThe bones of your dead lords and honour them\nWith treble ceremony, rather than a gap\nShould be in their dear rites, we would supply ’t,\nBut those we will depute which shall invest\nYou in your dignities and even each thing\nOur haste does leave imperfect. So, adieu,\nAnd heaven’s good eyes look on you.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Queens.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Herald and Soldiers bearing\nPalamon and Arcite\non hearses.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What are those?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Men of great quality, as may be judged\nBy their appointment. Some of Thebes have told ’s\nThey are sisters’ children, nephews to the King.","speaker":"HERALD","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By th’ helm of Mars, I saw them in the war,\nLike to a pair of lions, smeared with prey,\nMake lanes in troops aghast. I fixed my note\nConstantly on them, for they were a mark\nWorth a god’s view. What prisoner was ’t that told me\nWhen I enquired their names?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wi’ leave, they’re called Arcite and Palamon.","speaker":"HERALD","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis right; those, those. They are not dead?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor in a state of life. Had they been taken\nWhen their last hurts were given, ’twas possible\nThey might have been recovered; yet they breathe\nAnd have the name of men.","speaker":"HERALD","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then like men use ’em.\nThe very lees of such, millions of rates,\nExceed the wine of others. All our surgeons\nConvent in their behoof; our richest balms,\nRather than niggard, waste. Their lives concern us\nMuch more than Thebes is worth. Rather than have ’em\nFreed of this plight, and in their morning state,\nSound and at liberty, I would ’em dead;\nBut forty-thousandfold we had rather have ’em\nPrisoners to us than death. Bear ’em speedily\nFrom our kind air, to them unkind, and minister\nWhat man to man may do, for our sake, more,\nSince I have known frights, fury, friends’ behests,\nLove’s provocations, zeal, a mistress’ task,\nDesire of liberty, a fever, madness,\nHath set a mark which nature could not reach to\nWithout some imposition, sickness in will\nO’er-wrestling strength in reason. For our love\nAnd great Apollo’s mercy, all our best\nTheir best skill tender. Lead into the city,\nWhere, having bound things scattered, we will post\nTo Athens ’fore our army.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.4:15"}],"scene":4,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"1.5","label":"Act 1 · SCENE V. Another part of the same, more remote from Thebes","act":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Music. Enter the Queens with the hearses of their\nknights, in a funeral solemnity, &c.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Urns and odours bring away;\nVapours, sighs, darken the day;\nOur dole more deadly looks than dying;\nBalms and gums and heavy cheers,\nSacred vials filled with tears,\nAnd clamours through the wild air flying.","speaker":"SONG","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, all sad and solemn shows\nThat are quick-eyed Pleasure’s foes;\nWe convent naught else but woes.\nWe convent naught else but woes.","speaker":"SONG","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This funeral path brings to your household’s grave.\nJoy seize on you again; peace sleep with him.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And this to yours.","speaker":"SECOND QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yours this way. Heavens lend\nA thousand differing ways to one sure end.","speaker":"FIRST QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This world’s a city full of straying streets,\nAnd death’s the market-place where each one meets.","speaker":"THIRD QUEEN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt severally.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:1.5:8"}],"scene":5,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"2.1","label":"Act 2 · SCENE I. Athens. A garden, with a castle in the background","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer and\nWooer.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I may depart with little while I live; something I may cast to you, not much.\nAlas, the prison I keep, though it be for great ones, yet they seldom come;\nbefore one salmon, you shall take a number of minnows. I am given out to be\nbetter lined than it can appear to me report is a true speaker. I would I were\nreally that I am delivered to be. Marry, what I have, be it what it will, I\nwill assure upon my daughter at the day of my death.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I demand no more than your own offer, and I will estate your daughter in\nwhat I have promised.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, we will talk more of this when the solemnity is past. But have you a full\npromise of her? When that shall be seen, I tender my consent.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Jailer’s Daughter, carrying rushes.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have sir. Here she comes.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your friend and I have chanced to name you here, upon the old business. But no\nmore of that now; so soon as the court hurry is over, we will have an end of\nit. I’ th’ meantime, look tenderly to the two prisoners. I can tell you they\nare princes.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These strewings are for their chamber. ’Tis pity they are in prison, and ’twere\npity they should be out. I do think they have patience to make any adversity\nashamed. The prison itself is proud of ’em, and they have all the world in\ntheir chamber.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are famed to be a pair of absolute men.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my troth, I think fame but stammers ’em; they stand a grise above the reach\nof report.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I heard them reported in the battle to be the only doers.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, most likely, for they are noble sufferers. I marvel how they would have\nlooked had they been victors, that with such a constant nobility enforce a\nfreedom out of bondage, making misery their mirth and affliction a toy to jest\nat.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do they so?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It seems to me they have no more sense of their captivity than I of ruling\nAthens. They eat well, look merrily, discourse of many things, but nothing of\ntheir own restraint and disasters. Yet sometime a divided sigh, martyred as\n’twere i’ th’ deliverance, will break from one of them—when the other presently\ngives it so sweet a rebuke that I could wish myself a sigh to be so chid, or at\nleast a sigher to be comforted.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I never saw ’em.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Duke himself came privately in the night, and so did they.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and\nArcite, above.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What the reason of it is, I know not. Look, yonder they are; that’s Arcite\nlooks out.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, sir, no, that’s Palamon. Arcite is the lower of the twain; you may perceive\na part of him.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to, leave your pointing; they would not make us their object. Out of their\nsight.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is a holiday to look on them. Lord, the difference of men!","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.1:22"}],"scene":1},{"id":"2.2","label":"Act 2 · SCENE II. The prison","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and\nArcite in prison.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How do you, noble cousin?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How do you, sir?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, strong enough to laugh at misery\nAnd bear the chance of war; yet we are prisoners\nI fear for ever, cousin.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I believe it,\nAnd to that destiny have patiently\nLaid up my hour to come.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, cousin Arcite,\nWhere is Thebes now? Where is our noble country?\nWhere are our friends and kindreds? Never more\nMust we behold those comforts, never see\nThe hardy youths strive for the games of honour,\nHung with the painted favours of their ladies,\nLike tall ships under sail; then start amongst ’em,\nAnd as an east wind leave ’em all behind us,\nLike lazy clouds, whilst Palamon and Arcite,\nEven in the wagging of a wanton leg,\nOutstripped the people’s praises, won the garlands,\nEre they have time to wish ’em ours. O, never\nShall we two exercise, like twins of honour,\nOur arms again, and feel our fiery horses\nLike proud seas under us! Our good swords now—\nBetter the red-eyed god of war ne’er wore—\nRavished our sides, like age must run to rust\nAnd deck the temples of those gods that hate us;\nThese hands shall never draw ’em out like lightning\nTo blast whole armies more.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, Palamon,\nThose hopes are prisoners with us. Here we are,\nAnd here the graces of our youths must wither\nLike a too-timely spring; here age must find us\nAnd, which is heaviest, Palamon, unmarried.\nThe sweet embraces of a loving wife,\nLoaden with kisses, armed with thousand Cupids,\nShall never clasp our necks; no issue know us,\nNo figures of ourselves shall we e’er see,\nTo glad our age, and like young eagles teach ’em\nBoldly to gaze against bright arms and say\n“Remember what your fathers were, and conquer!”\nThe fair-eyed maids shall weep our banishments\nAnd in their songs curse ever-blinded Fortune\nTill she for shame see what a wrong she has done\nTo youth and nature. This is all our world.\nWe shall know nothing here but one another,\nHear nothing but the clock that tells our woes.\nThe vine shall grow, but we shall never see it;\nSummer shall come, and with her all delights,\nBut dead-cold winter must inhabit here still.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis too true, Arcite. To our Theban hounds\nThat shook the aged forest with their echoes\nNo more now must we hallow, no more shake\nOur pointed javelins whilst the angry swine\nFlies like a Parthian quiver from our rages,\nStruck with our well-steeled darts. All valiant uses,\nThe food and nourishment of noble minds,\nIn us two here shall perish; we shall die,\nWhich is the curse of honour, lastly,\nChildren of grief and ignorance.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet, cousin,\nEven from the bottom of these miseries,\nFrom all that fortune can inflict upon us,\nI see two comforts rising, two mere blessings,\nIf the gods please: to hold here a brave patience,\nAnd the enjoying of our griefs together.\nWhilst Palamon is with me, let me perish\nIf I think this our prison!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Certainly\n’Tis a main goodness, cousin, that our fortunes\nWere twined together; ’tis most true, two souls\nPut in two noble bodies, let ’em suffer\nThe gall of hazard, so they grow together,\nWill never sink; they must not, say they could.\nA willing man dies sleeping and all’s done.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall we make worthy uses of this place\nThat all men hate so much?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How, gentle cousin?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let’s think this prison holy sanctuary,\nTo keep us from corruption of worse men.\nWe are young and yet desire the ways of honour;\nThat liberty and common conversation,\nThe poison of pure spirits, might like women,\nWoo us to wander from. What worthy blessing\nCan be but our imaginations\nMay make it ours? And here being thus together,\nWe are an endless mine to one another;\nWe are one another’s wife, ever begetting\nNew births of love; we are father, friends, acquaintance;\nWe are, in one another, families;\nI am your heir, and you are mine. This place\nIs our inheritance; no hard oppressor\nDare take this from us; here with a little patience\nWe shall live long and loving. No surfeits seek us;\nThe hand of war hurts none here, nor the seas\nSwallow their youth. Were we at liberty,\nA wife might part us lawfully, or business;\nQuarrels consume us; envy of ill men\nCrave our acquaintance. I might sicken, cousin,\nWhere you should never know it, and so perish\nWithout your noble hand to close mine eyes,\nOr prayers to the gods. A thousand chances,\nWere we from hence, would sever us.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have made me—\nI thank you, cousin Arcite—almost wanton\nWith my captivity. What a misery\nIt is to live abroad and everywhere!\n’Tis like a beast, methinks. I find the court here,\nI am sure, a more content; and all those pleasures\nThat woo the wills of men to vanity\nI see through now, and am sufficient\nTo tell the world ’tis but a gaudy shadow\nThat old Time as he passes by takes with him.\nWhat had we been, old in the court of Creon,\nWhere sin is justice, lust and ignorance\nThe virtues of the great ones? Cousin Arcite,\nHad not the loving gods found this place for us,\nWe had died as they do, ill old men, unwept,\nAnd had their epitaphs, the people’s curses.\nShall I say more?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would hear you still.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ye shall.\nIs there record of any two that loved\nBetter than we do, Arcite?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sure, there cannot.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not think it possible our friendship\nShould ever leave us.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Till our deaths it cannot;","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Emilia and her\nWoman, below.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And after death our spirits shall be led\nTo those that love eternally. Speak on, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This garden has a world of pleasures in’t.\nWhat flower is this?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis called narcissus, madam.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That was a fair boy, certain, but a fool,\nTo love himself. Were there not maids enough?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, forward.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or were they all hard-hearted?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They could not be to one so fair.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou wouldst not.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think I should not, madam.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s a good wench.\nBut take heed to your kindness, though.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, madam?","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Men are mad things.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will ye go forward, cousin?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Canst not thou work such flowers in silk, wench?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll have a gown full of ’em, and of these.\nThis is a pretty colour; will ’t not do\nRarely upon a skirt, wench?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dainty, madam.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousin, cousin! How do you, sir? Why, Palamon!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never till now I was in prison, Arcite.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, what’s the matter, man?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Behold, and wonder!\nBy heaven, she is a goddess.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ha!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do reverence. She is a goddess, Arcite.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of all flowers,\nMethinks a rose is best.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, gentle madam?","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is the very emblem of a maid.\nFor when the west wind courts her gently,\nHow modestly she blows and paints the sun\nWith her chaste blushes! When the north comes near her,\nRude and impatient, then, like chastity,\nShe locks her beauties in her bud again,\nAnd leaves him to base briers.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet, good madam,\nSometimes her modesty will blow so far\nShe falls for ’t. A maid,\nIf she have any honour, would be loath\nTo take example by her.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou art wanton.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is wondrous fair.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is all the beauty extant.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The sun grows high; let’s walk in. Keep these flowers.\nWe’ll see how near art can come near their colours.\nI am wondrous merry-hearted. I could laugh now.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I could lie down, I am sure.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And take one with you?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s as we bargain, madam.","speaker":"WOMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, agree then.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:56"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Emilia and\nWoman.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What think you of this beauty?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis a rare one.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is’t but a rare one?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, a matchless beauty.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Might not a man well lose himself, and love her?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot tell what you have done; I have,\nBeshrew mine eyes for’t! Now I feel my shackles.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You love her, then?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who would not?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And desire her?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Before my liberty.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I saw her first.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s nothing.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But it shall be.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I saw her too.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, but you must not love her.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will not, as you do, to worship her\nAs she is heavenly and a blessed goddess.\nI love her as a woman, to enjoy her.\nSo both may love.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall not love at all.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not love at all! Who shall deny me?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I, that first saw her; I that took possession\nFirst with mine eye of all those beauties in her\nRevealed to mankind. If thou lovest her,\nOr entertain’st a hope to blast my wishes,\nThou art a traitor, Arcite, and a fellow\nFalse as thy title to her. Friendship, blood,\nAnd all the ties between us, I disclaim\nIf thou once think upon her.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, I love her;\nAnd, if the lives of all my name lay on it,\nI must do so; I love her with my soul.\nIf that will lose ye, farewell, Palamon.\nI say again, I love, and in loving her maintain\nI am as worthy and as free a lover\nAnd have as just a title to her beauty,\nAs any Palamon, or any living\nThat is a man’s son.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have I called thee friend?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, and have found me so. Why are you moved thus?\nLet me deal coldly with you: am not I\nPart of your blood, part of your soul? You have told me\nThat I was Palamon and you were Arcite.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Am not I liable to those affections,\nThose joys, griefs, angers, fears, my friend shall suffer?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ye may be.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why then would you deal so cunningly,\nSo strangely, so unlike a noble kinsman,\nTo love alone? Speak truly; do you think me\nUnworthy of her sight?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No; but unjust,\nIf thou pursue that sight.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because another\nFirst sees the enemy, shall I stand still\nAnd let mine honour down, and never charge?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, if he be but one.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But say that one\nHad rather combat me?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let that one say so,\nAnd use thy freedom. Else, if thou pursuest her,\nBe as that cursed man that hates his country,\nA branded villain.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are mad.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must be,\nTill thou art worthy, Arcite; it concerns me;\nAnd in this madness, if I hazard thee\nAnd take thy life, I deal but truely.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie, sir!\nYou play the child extremely. I will love her;\nI must, I ought to do so, and I dare,\nAnd all this justly.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, that now, that now,\nThy false self and thy friend had but this fortune,\nTo be one hour at liberty, and grasp\nOur good swords in our hands! I would quickly teach thee\nWhat ’twere to filch affection from another!\nThou art baser in it than a cutpurse.\nPut but thy head out of this window more\nAnd, as I have a soul, I’ll nail thy life to ’t.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou dar’st not, fool, thou canst not, thou art feeble.\nPut my head out? I’ll throw my body out\nAnd leap the garden, when I see her next\nAnd pitch between her arms, to anger thee.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:93"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more; the keeper’s coming. I shall live\nTo knock thy brains out with my shackles.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By your leave, gentlemen.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, honest keeper?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Arcite, you must presently to th’ Duke;\nThe cause I know not yet.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am ready, keeper.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Prince Palamon, I must awhile bereave you\nOf your fair cousin’s company.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:101"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Arcite and\nJailer.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And me too,\nEven when you please, of life.—Why is he sent for?\nIt may be he shall marry her; he’s goodly,\nAnd like enough the Duke hath taken notice\nBoth of his blood and body. But his falsehood!\nWhy should a friend be treacherous? If that\nGet him a wife so noble and so fair,\nLet honest men ne’er love again. Once more\nI would but see this fair one. Blessed garden\nAnd fruit and flowers more blessed that still blossom\nAs her bright eyes shine on ye! Would I were,\nFor all the fortune of my life hereafter,\nYon little tree, yon blooming apricock!\nHow I would spread and fling my wanton arms\nIn at her window! I would bring her fruit\nFit for the gods to feed on; youth and pleasure\nStill as she tasted should be doubled on her;\nAnd, if she be not heavenly, I would make her\nSo near the gods in nature, they should fear her.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:103"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And then I am sure she would love me. How now, keeper?\nWhere’s Arcite?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Banished. Prince Pirithous\nObtained his liberty, but never more\nUpon his oath and life must he set foot\nUpon this kingdom.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’s a blessed man.\nHe shall see Thebes again, and call to arms\nThe bold young men that, when he bids ’em charge,\nFall on like fire. Arcite shall have a fortune,\nIf he dare make himself a worthy lover,\nYet in the field to strike a battle for her;\nAnd, if he lose her then, he’s a cold coward.\nHow bravely may he bear himself to win her\nIf he be noble Arcite, thousand ways!\nWere I at liberty, I would do things\nOf such a virtuous greatness that this lady,\nThis blushing virgin, should take manhood to her\nAnd seek to ravish me.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord for you\nI have this charge to—","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To discharge my life?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, but from this place to remove your lordship;\nThe windows are too open.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Devils take ’em,\nThat are so envious to me! Prithee, kill me.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And hang for’t afterward!","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By this good light,\nHad I a sword I would kill thee.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, my Lord?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou bringst such pelting, scurvy news continually,\nThou art not worthy life. I will not go.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, you must, my lord.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May I see the garden?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then I am resolved, I will not go.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must constrain you then; and, for you are dangerous,\nI’ll clap more irons on you.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, good keeper.\nI’ll shake ’em so, ye shall not sleep;\nI’ll make you a new morris. Must I go?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is no remedy.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, kind window.\nMay rude wind never hurt thee!—O, my lady,\nIf ever thou hast felt what sorrow was,\nDream how I suffer.—Come, now bury me.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:123"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Palamon and\nJailer.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.2:124"}],"scene":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"2.3","label":"Act 2 · SCENE III. The country near Athens","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Arcite.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Banished the kingdom? ’Tis a benefit,\nA mercy I must thank ’em for; but banished\nThe free enjoying of that face I die for,\nO, ’twas a studied punishment, a death\nBeyond imagination, such a vengeance\nThat, were I old and wicked, all my sins\nCould never pluck upon me. Palamon,\nThou hast the start now; thou shalt stay and see\nHer bright eyes break each morning ’gainst thy window\nAnd let in life into thee; thou shalt feed\nUpon the sweetness of a noble beauty\nThat nature ne’er exceeded nor ne’er shall.\nGood gods, what happiness has Palamon!\nTwenty to one, he’ll come to speak to her;\nAnd if she be as gentle as she’s fair,\nI know she’s his; he has a tongue will tame\nTempests and make the wild rocks wanton.\nCome what can come,\nThe worst is death; I will not leave the kingdom.\nI know mine own is but a heap of ruins,\nAnd no redress there. If I go, he has her.\nI am resolved another shape shall make me\nOr end my fortunes. Either way I am happy.\nI’ll see her and be near her, or no more.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter four Countrymen, and one with a garland\nbefore them.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My masters, I’ll be there, that’s certain.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I’ll be there.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then, have with you, boys. ’Tis but a chiding.\nLet the plough play today; I’ll tickle ’t out\nOf the jades’ tails tomorrow.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sure\nTo have my wife as jealous as a turkey,\nBut that’s all one. I’ll go through; let her mumble.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Clap her aboard tomorrow night, and stow her,\nAnd all’s made up again.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, do but put\nA fescue in her fist and you shall see her\nTake a new lesson out and be a good wench.\nDo we all hold against the Maying?","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold?\nWhat should ail us?","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arcas will be there.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And Sennois.\nAnd Rycas; and three better lads ne’er danced\nUnder green tree. And ye know what wenches, ha?\nBut will the dainty domine, the schoolmaster,\nKeep touch, do you think? For he does all, ye know.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’ll eat a hornbook ere he fail. Go to;\nThe matter’s too far driven between him\nAnd the tanner’s daughter to let slip now;\nAnd she must see the Duke, and she must dance too.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall we be lusty?","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All the boys in Athens\nBlow wind i’ th’ breech on ’s. And here I’ll be,\nAnd there I’ll be, for our town, and here again,\nAnd there again. Ha, boys, hey for the weavers!","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This must be done i’ th’ woods.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, pardon me.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By any means; our thing of learning says so—\nWhere he himself will edify the Duke\nMost parlously in our behalfs. He’s excellent i’ th’ woods;\nBring him to th’ plains, his learning makes no cry.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We’ll see the sports, then every man to ’s tackle;\nAnd, sweet companions, let’s rehearse, by any means,\nBefore the ladies see us, and do sweetly,\nAnd God knows what may come on ’t.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Content; the sports once ended, we’ll perform.\nAway, boys, and hold.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By your leaves, honest friends: pray you, whither go you?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whither? Why, what a question’s that?","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, ’tis a question\nTo me that know not.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To the games, my friend.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where were you bred, you know it not?","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not far, sir;\nAre there such games today?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, marry, are there,\nAnd such as you never saw; the Duke himself\nWill be in person there.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What pastimes are they?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wrestling, and running.—’Tis a pretty fellow.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou wilt not go along?","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not yet, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir,\nTake your own time. Come, boys.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My mind misgives me,\nThis fellow has a vengeance trick o’ th’ hip;\nMark how his body’s made for ’t.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll be hanged, though,\nIf he dare venture. Hang him, plum porridge!\nHe wrestle? He roast eggs! Come, let’s be gone, lads.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Countrymen.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is an offered opportunity\nI durst not wish for. Well I could have wrestled—\nThe best men called it excellent—and run\nSwifter than wind upon a field of corn,\nCurling the wealthy ears, never flew. I’ll venture,\nAnd in some poor disguise be there. Who knows\nWhether my brows may not be girt with garlands,\nAnd happiness prefer me to a place\nWhere I may ever dwell in sight of her?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Arcite.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.3:38"}],"scene":3,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"2.4","label":"Act 2 · SCENE IV. Athens. A room in the prison","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter alone.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should I love this gentleman? ’Tis odds\nHe never will affect me. I am base,\nMy father the mean keeper of his prison,\nAnd he a prince. To marry him is hopeless;\nTo be his whore is witless. Out upon ’t!\nWhat pushes are we wenches driven to\nWhen fifteen once has found us! First, I saw him;\nI, seeing, thought he was a goodly man;\nHe has as much to please a woman in him,\nIf he please to bestow it so, as ever\nThese eyes yet looked on. Next, I pitied him,\nAnd so would any young wench, o’ my conscience,\nThat ever dreamed, or vowed her maidenhead\nTo a young handsome man. Then I loved him,\nExtremely loved him, infinitely loved him!\nAnd yet he had a cousin, fair as he too,\nBut in my heart was Palamon, and there,\nLord, what a coil he keeps! To hear him\nSing in an evening, what a heaven it is!\nAnd yet his songs are sad ones. Fairer spoken\nWas never gentleman. When I come in\nTo bring him water in a morning, first\nHe bows his noble body, then salutes me thus:\n“Fair, gentle maid, good morrow. May thy goodness\nGet thee a happy husband.” Once he kissed me;\nI loved my lips the better ten days after.\nWould he would do so ev’ry day! He grieves much—\nAnd me as much to see his misery.\nWhat should I do to make him know I love him?\nFor I would fain enjoy him. Say I ventured\nTo set him free? What says the law then?\nThus much for law or kindred! I will do it;\nAnd this night, or tomorrow, he shall love me.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.4:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.4:3"}],"scene":4,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"2.5","label":"Act 2 · SCENE V. An open place in Athens","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"A short flourish of cornets and shouts within. Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Pirithous, Emilia; Arcite in\ndisguise as a countryman, with a garland, Attendants, and others.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have done worthily. I have not seen,\nSince Hercules, a man of tougher sinews.\nWhate’er you are, you run the best and wrestle,\nThat these times can allow.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am proud to please you.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What country bred you?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This; but far off, Prince.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you a gentleman?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My father said so;\nAnd to those gentle uses gave me life.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you his heir?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His youngest, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your father\nSure is a happy sire then. What profess you?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A little of all noble qualities.\nI could have kept a hawk and well have hallowed\nTo a deep cry of dogs. I dare not praise\nMy feat in horsemanship, yet they that knew me\nWould say it was my best piece; last, and greatest,\nI would be thought a soldier.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are perfect.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon my soul, a proper man.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is so.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How do you like him, lady?","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I admire him.\nI have not seen so young a man so noble,\nIf he say true, of his sort.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Believe,\nHis mother was a wondrous handsome woman;\nHis face, methinks, goes that way.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But his body\nAnd fiery mind illustrate a brave father.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mark how his virtue, like a hidden sun,\nBreaks through his baser garments.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’s well got, sure.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What made you seek this place, sir?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noble Theseus,\nTo purchase name and do my ablest service\nTo such a well-found wonder as thy worth;\nFor only in thy court, of all the world,\nDwells fair-eyed Honour.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All his words are worthy.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, we are much indebted to your travel,\nNor shall you lose your wish.—Pirithous,\nDispose of this fair gentleman.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thanks, Theseus.\nWhate’er you are, you’re mine, and I shall give you\nTo a most noble service: to this lady,\nThis bright young virgin; pray, observe her goodness.\nYou have honoured her fair birthday with your virtues,\nAnd, as your due, you’re hers; kiss her fair hand, sir.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, you’re a noble giver.—Dearest beauty,\nThus let me seal my vowed faith.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[He kisses her hand.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When your servant,\nYour most unworthy creature, but offends you,\nCommand him die, he shall.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That were too cruel.\nIf you deserve well, sir, I shall soon see ’t.\nYou’re mine, and somewhat better than your rank\nI’ll use you.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll see you furnished, and because you say\nYou are a horseman, I must needs entreat you\nThis afternoon to ride, but ’tis a rough one.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I like him better, Prince; I shall not then\nFreeze in my saddle.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet, you must be ready,—\nAnd you, Emilia,—and you, friend,—and all,\nTomorrow by the sun, to do observance\nTo flowery May, in Dian’s wood.—Wait well, sir,\nUpon your mistress.—Emily, I hope\nHe shall not go afoot.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That were a shame, sir,\nWhile I have horses.—Take your choice, and what\nYou want at any time, let me but know it.\nIf you serve faithfully, I dare assure you\nYou’ll find a loving mistress.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I do not,\nLet me find that my father ever hated,\nDisgrace and blows.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go lead the way; you have won it.\nIt shall be so; you shall receive all dues\nFit for the honour you have won; ’twere wrong else.\nSister, beshrew my heart, you have a servant,\nThat, if I were a woman, would be master.\nBut you are wise.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope too wise for that, sir.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:36"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.5:37"}],"scene":5,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"2.6","label":"Act 2 · SCENE VI. Athens. Before the prison","act":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter alone.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let all the dukes and all the devils roar,\nHe is at liberty! I have ventured for him\nAnd out I have brought him; to a little wood\nA mile hence I have sent him, where a cedar\nHigher than all the rest spreads like a plane\nFast by a brook, and there he shall keep close\nTill I provide him files and food, for yet\nHis iron bracelets are not off. O Love,\nWhat a stout-hearted child thou art! My father\nDurst better have endured cold iron than done it.\nI love him beyond love and beyond reason,\nOr wit, or safety. I have made him know it;\nI care not, I am desperate. If the law\nFind me and then condemn me for ’t, some wenches,\nSome honest-hearted maids, will sing my dirge\nAnd tell to memory my death was noble,\nDying almost a martyr. That way he takes,\nI purpose is my way too. Sure he cannot\nBe so unmanly as to leave me here.\nIf he do, maids will not so easily\nTrust men again. And yet he has not thanked me\nFor what I have done; no, not so much as kissed me,\nAnd that, methinks, is not so well; nor scarcely\nCould I persuade him to become a free man,\nHe made such scruples of the wrong he did\nTo me and to my father. Yet I hope,\nWhen he considers more, this love of mine\nWill take more root within him. Let him do\nWhat he will with me, so he use me kindly;\nFor use me so he shall, or I’ll proclaim him,\nAnd to his face, no man. I’ll presently\nProvide him necessaries and pack my clothes up,\nAnd where there is a path of ground I’ll venture,\nSo he be with me. By him, like a shadow\nI’ll ever dwell. Within this hour the hubbub\nWill be all o’er the prison. I am then\nKissing the man they look for. Farewell, father!\nGet many more such prisoners and such daughters,\nAnd shortly you may keep yourself. Now to him.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.6:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:2.6:3"}],"scene":6,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"3.1","label":"Act 3 · SCENE I. A forest near Athens","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Cornets in sundry places. Noise and hallowing as people a-Maying. Enter Arcite alone.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Duke has lost Hippolyta; each took\nA several land. This is a solemn rite\nThey owe bloomed May, and the Athenians pay it\nTo th’ heart of ceremony. O Queen Emilia,\nFresher than May, sweeter\nThan her gold buttons on the boughs, or all\nTh’ enameled knacks o’ th’ mead or garden—yea,\nWe challenge too the bank of any nymph\nThat makes the stream seem flowers; thou, O jewel\nO’ th’ wood, o’ th’ world, hast likewise blessed a pace\nWith thy sole presence. In thy rumination\nThat I, poor man, might eftsoons come between\nAnd chop on some cold thought! Thrice blessed chance\nTo drop on such a mistress, expectation\nMost guiltless on ’t. Tell me, O Lady Fortune,\nNext after Emily my sovereign, how far\nI may be proud. She takes strong note of me,\nHath made me near her, and this beauteous morn,\nThe prim’st of all the year, presents me with\nA brace of horses; two such steeds might well\nBe by a pair of kings backed, in a field\nThat their crowns’ titles tried. Alas, alas,\nPoor cousin Palamon, poor prisoner, thou\nSo little dream’st upon my fortune that\nThou think’st thyself the happier thing, to be\nSo near Emilia; me thou deem’st at Thebes,\nAnd therein wretched, although free. But if\nThou knew’st my mistress breathed on me, and that\nI eared her language, lived in her eye, O coz,\nWhat passion would enclose thee!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon as out of a bush, with his shackles;\nhe bends his fist at Arcite.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Traitor kinsman,\nThou shouldst perceive my passion, if these signs\nOf prisonment were off me, and this hand\nBut owner of a sword. By all oaths in one,\nI and the justice of my love would make thee\nA confessed traitor! O thou most perfidious\nThat ever gently looked, the void’st of honour\nThat e’er bore gentle token, falsest cousin\nThat ever blood made kin! Call’st thou her thine?\nI’ll prove it in my shackles, with these hands,\nVoid of appointment, that thou liest, and art\nA very thief in love, a chaffy lord,\nNor worth the name of villain. Had I a sword,\nAnd these house-clogs away—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dear cousin Palamon—","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cozener Arcite, give me language such\nAs thou hast showed me feat.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not finding in\nThe circuit of my breast any gross stuff\nTo form me like your blazon holds me to\nThis gentleness of answer. ’Tis your passion\nThat thus mistakes, the which, to you being enemy,\nCannot to me be kind. Honour and honesty\nI cherish and depend on, howsoe’er\nYou skip them in me, and with them, fair coz,\nI’ll maintain my proceedings. Pray be pleased\nTo show in generous terms your griefs, since that\nYour question’s with your equal, who professes\nTo clear his own way with the mind and sword\nOf a true gentleman.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That thou durst, Arcite!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My coz, my coz, you have been well advertised\nHow much I dare; you’ve seen me use my sword\nAgainst th’ advice of fear. Sure, of another\nYou would not hear me doubted, but your silence\nShould break out, though i’ th’ sanctuary.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir,\nI have seen you move in such a place, which well\nMight justify your manhood; you were called\nA good knight and a bold. But the whole week’s not fair\nIf any day it rain. Their valiant temper\nMen lose when they incline to treachery;\nAnd then they fight like compelled bears, would fly\nWere they not tied.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Kinsman, you might as well\nSpeak this and act it in your glass as to\nHis ear which now disdains you.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come up to me;\nQuit me of these cold gyves, give me a sword\nThough it be rusty, and the charity\nOf one meal lend me. Come before me then,\nA good sword in thy hand, and do but say\nThat Emily is thine, I will forgive\nThe trespass thou hast done me, yea, my life,\nIf then thou carry ’t; and brave souls in shades\nThat have died manly, which will seek of me\nSome news from earth, they shall get none but this:\nThat thou art brave and noble.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be content.\nAgain betake you to your hawthorn house.\nWith counsel of the night, I will be here\nWith wholesome viands. These impediments\nWill I file off; you shall have garments and\nPerfumes to kill the smell o’ th’ prison. After,\nWhen you shall stretch yourself and say but “Arcite,\nI am in plight,” there shall be at your choice\nBoth sword and armour.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh you heavens, dares any\nSo noble bear a guilty business? None\nBut only Arcite, therefore none but Arcite\nIn this kind is so bold.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet Palamon.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do embrace you and your offer; for\nYour offer do ’t I only, sir; your person,\nWithout hypocrisy I may not wish\nMore than my sword’s edge on ’t.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Wind horns of cornets.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You hear the horns.\nEnter your musit, lest this match between ’s\nBe crossed ere met. Give me your hand; farewell.\nI’ll bring you every needful thing. I pray you,\nTake comfort and be strong.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray hold your promise,\nAnd do the deed with a bent brow. Most certain\nYou love me not; be rough with me, and pour\nThis oil out of your language. By this air,\nI could for each word give a cuff, my stomach\nNot reconciled by reason.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Plainly spoken.\nYet pardon me hard language. When I spur\nMy horse, I chide him not; content and anger\nIn me have but one face.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Wind horns.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark, sir, they call\nThe scattered to the banquet. You must guess\nI have an office there.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, your attendance\nCannot please heaven, and I know your office\nUnjustly is achieved.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis a good title.\nI am persuaded, this question, sick between ’s,\nBy bleeding must be cured. I am a suitor\nThat to your sword you will bequeath this plea,\nAnd talk of it no more.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But this one word:\nYou are going now to gaze upon my mistress,\nFor, note you, mine she is—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then—","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, pray you,\nYou talk of feeding me to breed me strength.\nYou are going now to look upon a sun\nThat strengthens what it looks on; there\nYou have a vantage o’er me. But enjoy ’t till\nI may enforce my remedy. Farewell.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.1:28"}],"scene":1},{"id":"3.2","label":"Act 3 · SCENE II. Another Part of the forest","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter alone.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He has mistook the brake I meant, is gone\nAfter his fancy. ’Tis now well-nigh morning.\nNo matter; would it were perpetual night,\nAnd darkness lord o’ th’ world. Hark, ’tis a wolf!\nIn me hath grief slain fear, and but for one thing,\nI care for nothing, and that’s Palamon.\nI reck not if the wolves would jaw me, so\nHe had this file. What if I hallowed for him?\nI cannot hallow. If I whooped, what then?\nIf he not answered, I should call a wolf,\nAnd do him but that service. I have heard\nStrange howls this livelong night; why may ’t not be\nThey have made prey of him? He has no weapons;\nHe cannot run; the jingling of his gyves\nMight call fell things to listen, who have in them\nA sense to know a man unarmed and can\nSmell where resistance is. I’ll set it down\nHe’s torn to pieces; they howled many together,\nAnd then they fed on him. So much for that.\nBe bold to ring the bell. How stand I then?\nAll’s chared when he is gone. No, no, I lie.\nMy father’s to be hanged for his escape;\nMyself to beg, if I prized life so much\nAs to deny my act; but that I would not,\nShould I try death by dozens. I am moped.\nFood took I none these two days;\nSipped some water. I have not closed mine eyes\nSave when my lids scoured off their brine. Alas,\nDissolve, my life! Let not my sense unsettle,\nLest I should drown, or stab, or hang myself.\nO state of nature, fail together in me,\nSince thy best props are warped! So, which way now?\nThe best way is the next way to a grave;\nEach errant step beside is torment. Lo,\nThe moon is down, the crickets chirp, the screech owl\nCalls in the dawn. All offices are done\nSave what I fail in. But the point is this:\nAn end, and that is all.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.2:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.2:3"}],"scene":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"3.3","label":"Act 3 · SCENE III. The same part of the forest as in scene I.","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Arcite with meat, wine and files.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I should be near the place.—Ho! Cousin Palamon!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[From the bush.] Arcite?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The same. I have brought you food and files.\nCome forth and fear not; here’s no Theseus.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor none so honest, Arcite.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s no matter.\nWe’ll argue that hereafter. Come, take courage;\nYou shall not die thus beastly. Here, sir, drink—\nI know you are faint—then I’ll talk further with you.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arcite, thou mightst now poison me.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I might;\nBut I must fear you first. Sit down and, good now,\nNo more of these vain parleys; let us not,\nHaving our ancient reputation with us,\nMake talk for fools and cowards. To your health.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Drinks.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray sit down, then, and let me entreat you,\nBy all the honesty and honour in you,\nNo mention of this woman; ’twill disturb us.\nWe shall have time enough.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir, I’ll pledge you.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Drinks.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Drink a good hearty draught; it breeds good blood, man.\nDo not you feel it thaw you?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, I’ll tell you\nAfter a draught or two more.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Spare it not; the Duke has more, coz. Eat now.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Eats.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am glad you have so good a stomach.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am gladder I have so good meat to ’t.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is’t not mad lodging,\nHere in the wild woods, cousin?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, for them\nThat have wild consciences.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How tastes your victuals?\nYour hunger needs no sauce, I see.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not much.\nBut if it did, yours is too tart, sweet cousin.\nWhat is this?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Venison.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis a lusty meat.\nGive me more wine. Here, Arcite, to the wenches\nWe have known in our days! The Lord Steward’s daughter,\nDo you remember her?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"After you, coz.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She loved a black-haired man.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She did so; well, sir?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I have heard some call him Arcite, and—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Out with’t, faith.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She met him in an arbour.\nWhat did she there, coz? Play o’ th’ virginals?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Something she did, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Made her groan a month for ’t,\nOr two, or three, or ten.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Marshal’s sister\nHad her share too, as I remember, cousin,\nElse there be tales abroad. You’ll pledge her?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A pretty brown wench ’tis. There was a time\nWhen young men went a-hunting, and a wood,\nAnd a broad beech; and thereby hangs a tale.\nHeigh ho!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For Emily, upon my life! Fool,\nAway with this strained mirth! I say again\nThat sigh was breathed for Emily. Base cousin,\nDar’st thou break first?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are wide.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By heaven and earth,\nThere’s nothing in thee honest.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then I’ll leave you.\nYou are a beast now.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As thou mak’st me, traitor.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There’s all things needful: files and shirts and perfumes.\nI’ll come again some two hours hence, and bring\nThat that shall quiet all.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A sword and armour?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fear me not. You are now too foul. Farewell.\nGet off your trinkets; you shall want naught.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirrah—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll hear no more.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If he keep touch, he dies for ’t.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.3:51"}],"scene":3,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"3.4","label":"Act 3 · SCENE IV. Another part of the forest","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am very cold, and all the stars are out too,\nThe little stars and all, that look like aglets.\nThe sun has seen my folly. Palamon!\nAlas, no; he’s in heaven. Where am I now?\nYonder’s the sea, and there’s a ship; how ’t tumbles!\nAnd there’s a rock lies watching under water;\nNow, now, it beats upon it; now, now, now,\nThere’s a leak sprung, a sound one! How they cry!\nRun her before the wind, you’ll lose all else.\nUp with a course or two, and tack about, boys!\nGood night, good night; you’re gone. I am very hungry.\nWould I could find a fine frog; he would tell me\nNews from all parts o’ th’ world; then would I make\nA carrack of a cockle shell, and sail\nBy east and north-east to the king of pygmies,\nFor he tells fortunes rarely. Now my father,\nTwenty to one, is trussed up in a trice\nTomorrow morning. I’ll say never a word.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.4:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Sings.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For I’ll cut my green coat a foot above my knee,\nAnd I’ll clip my yellow locks an inch below mine eye.\nHey nonny, nonny, nonny.\nHe’s buy me a white cut, forth for to ride,\nAnd I’ll go seek him through the world that is so wide.\nHey nonny, nonny, nonny.\nO, for a prick now, like a nightingale,\nTo put my breast against. I shall sleep like a top else.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.4:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.4:5"}],"scene":4,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"3.5","label":"Act 3 · SCENE V. Another part of the forest","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Schoolmaster and five\nCountrymen, one dressed as a Bavian.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie, fie,\nWhat tediosity and disinsanity\nIs here among ye! Have my rudiments\nBeen laboured so long with ye, milked unto ye,\nAnd, by a figure, even the very plum-broth\nAnd marrow of my understanding laid upon ye,\nAnd do you still cry “Where?” and “How?” and “Wherefore?”\nYou most coarse-frieze capacities, ye jean judgements,\nHave I said “Thus let be” and “There let be”\nAnd “Then let be” and no man understand me?\nProh Deum, medius fidius, ye are all dunces!\nFor why?\nHere stand I; here the Duke comes; there are you,\nClose in the thicket; the Duke appears; I meet him\nAnd unto him I utter learned things\nAnd many figures; he hears, and nods, and hums,\nAnd then cries “Rare!” and I go forward. At length\nI fling my cap up—mark there! Then do you\nAs once did Meleager and the boar,\nBreak comely out before him; like true lovers,\nCast yourselves in a body decently,\nAnd sweetly, by a figure, trace and turn, boys.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And sweetly we will do it, Master Gerald.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Draw up the company. Where’s the taborer?","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Timothy!","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, my mad boys, have at ye.","speaker":"TABORER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I say, where’s their women?","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter five Countrywomen.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here’s Friz and Maudlin.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And little Luce with the white legs, and bouncing Barbary.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And freckled Nel, that never failed her master.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where be your ribbons, maids? Swim with your bodies,\nAnd carry it sweetly and deliverly,\nAnd now and then a favour and a frisk.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us alone, sir.","speaker":"NEL","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where’s the rest o’ th’ music?","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dispersed, as you commanded.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Couple, then,\nAnd see what’s wanting. Where’s the Bavian?\nMy friend, carry your tail without offence\nOr scandal to the ladies; and be sure\nYou tumble with audacity and manhood;\nAnd when you bark, do it with judgement.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, sir.","speaker":"BAVIAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Quo usque tandem? Here is a woman wanting.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We may go whistle; all the fat’s i’ th’ fire.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have,\nas learned authors utter, washed a tile.\nwe have been fatuus and laboured vainly.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is that scornful piece, that scurvy hilding,\nThat gave her promise faithfully, she would be here,\nCicely, the sempster’s daughter.\nThe next gloves that I give her shall be dogskin!\nNay an she fail me once—You can tell, Arcas,\nShe swore by wine and bread, she would not break.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An eel and woman,\nA learned poet says, unless by th’ tail\nAnd with thy teeth thou hold, will either fail.\nIn manners this was false position.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A fire ill take her; does she flinch now?","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What\nShall we determine, sir?","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing.\nOur business is become a nullity,\nYea, and a woeful and a piteous nullity.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, when the credit of our town lay on it,\nNow to be frampul, now to piss o’ th’ nettle!\nGo thy ways; I’ll remember thee. I’ll fit thee.","speaker":"FOURTH COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Sings.]\nThe George Alow came from the south,\nFrom the coast of Barbary-a.\nAnd there he met with brave gallants of war,\nBy one, by two, by three-a.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well hailed, well hailed, you jolly gallants,\nAnd whither now are you bound-a?\nO let me have your company\nTill I come to the sound-a.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There was three fools fell out about an howlet:\nThe one said it was an owl,\nThe other he said nay,\nThe third he said it was a hawk,\nAnd her bells were cut away.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There’s a dainty mad woman, Master,\nComes i’ th’ nick, as mad as a March hare.\nIf we can get her dance, we are made again;\nI warrant her, she’ll do the rarest gambols.","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A madwoman? We are made, boys.","speaker":"FIRST COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And are you mad, good woman?","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would be sorry else.\nGive me your hand.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why?","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I can tell your fortune.\nYou are a fool. Tell ten. I have posed him. Buzz!\nFriend, you must eat no white bread; if you do,\nYour teeth will bleed extremely. Shall we dance, ho?\nI know you, you’re a tinker; sirrah tinker,\nStop no more holes but what you should.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dii boni! A tinker, damsel?","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or a conjurer.\nRaise me a devil now, and let him play\nQui passa o’ th’ bells and bones.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, take her,\nAnd fluently persuade her to a peace.\nEt opus exegi, quod nec Jovis ira, nec ignis—\nStrike up, and lead her in.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, lass, let’s trip it.","speaker":"SECOND COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll lead.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, do!","speaker":"THIRD COUNTRYMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Persuasively, and cunningly.\nAway, boys; I hear the horns. Give me some meditation,\nAnd mark your cue.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt all but Schoolmaster.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pallas inspire me.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:45"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Theseus, Pirithous, Hippolyta, Emilia, and\ntrain.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This way the stag took.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, and edify!","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What have we here?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some country sport, upon my life, sir.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir, go forward; we will “edify.”\nLadies, sit down. We’ll stay it.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou doughty Duke, all hail! All hail, sweet ladies!","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is a cold beginning.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you but favour, our country pastime made is.\nWe are a few of those collected here\nThat ruder tongues distinguish “villager.”\nAnd to say verity, and not to fable,\nWe are a merry rout, or else a rabble,\nOr company, or by a figure, chorus,\nThat ’fore thy dignity will dance a morris.\nAnd I that am the rectifier of all,\nBy title pædagogus, that let fall\nThe birch upon the breeches of the small ones,\nAnd humble with a ferula the tall ones,\nDo here present this machine, or this frame.\nAnd, dainty Duke, whose doughty dismal fame\nFrom Dis to Dædalus, from post to pillar,\nIs blown abroad, help me, thy poor well-willer,\nAnd with thy twinkling eyes look right and straight\nUpon this mighty Morr, of mickle weight.\nIs now comes in, which being glued together\nMakes Morris, and the cause that we came hither.\nThe body of our sport, of no small study.\nI first appear, though rude and raw and muddy,\nTo speak before thy noble grace this tenner,\nAt whose great feet I offer up my penner.\nThe next, the Lord of May and Lady bright,\nThe Chambermaid and Servingman, by night\nThat seek out silent hanging; then mine Host\nAnd his fat Spouse, that welcomes to their cost\nThe galled traveller, and with a beck’ning\nInforms the tapster to inflame the reck’ning.\nThen the beest-eating Clown and next the Fool,\nThe Bavian with long tail and eke long tool,\nCum multis aliis that make a dance.\nSay “Ay,” and all shall presently advance.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, ay, by any means, dear Domine.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Produce.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Intrate, filii! Come forth and foot it.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:57"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Music. Enter the Countrymen, Countrywomen and\nJailer’s Daughter; they perform a morris dance.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ladies, if we have been merry\nAnd have pleased ye with a derry,\nAnd a derry, and a down,\nSay the schoolmaster’s no clown.\nDuke, if we have pleased thee too\nAnd have done as good boys should do,\nGive us but a tree or twain\nFor a Maypole, and again,\nEre another year run out,\nWe’ll make thee laugh, and all this rout.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take twenty, Domine.—How does my sweetheart?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never so pleased, sir.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Twas an excellent dance,\nAnd, for a preface, I never heard a better.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Schoolmaster, I thank you.—One see’em all rewarded.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And here’s something to paint your pole withal.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:64"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[He gives money.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now to our sports again.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May the stag thou hunt’st stand long,\nAnd thy dogs be swift and strong;\nMay they kill him without lets,\nAnd the ladies eat his dowsets.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:67"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Theseus, Pirithous, Hippolyta, Emilia, Arcite\nand Train. Horns winded as they go out.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, we are all made. Dii deæque omnes,\nYou have danced rarely, wenches.","speaker":"SCHOOLMASTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:69"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.5:70"}],"scene":5,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"3.6","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VI. The same part of the forest as in scene III.","act":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon from the bush.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"About this hour my cousin gave his faith\nTo visit me again, and with him bring\nTwo swords and two good armours. If he fail,\nHe’s neither man nor soldier. When he left me,\nI did not think a week could have restored\nMy lost strength to me, I was grown so low\nAnd crestfall’n with my wants. I thank thee, Arcite,\nThou art yet a fair foe, and I feel myself,\nWith this refreshing, able once again\nTo outdure danger. To delay it longer\nWould make the world think, when it comes to hearing,\nThat I lay fatting like a swine to fight\nAnd not a soldier. Therefore, this blest morning\nShall be the last; and that sword he refuses,\nIf it but hold, I kill him with. ’Tis justice.\nSo, love and fortune for me!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Arcite with armours and swords.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, good morrow.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow, noble kinsman.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have put you\nTo too much pains, sir.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That too much, fair cousin,\nIs but a debt to honour, and my duty.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would you were so in all, sir; I could wish ye\nAs kind a kinsman as you force me find\nA beneficial foe, that my embraces\nMight thank ye, not my blows.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall think either,\nWell done, a noble recompence.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then I shall quit you.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Defy me in these fair terms, and you show\nMore than a mistress to me. No more anger,\nAs you love anything that’s honourable!\nWe were not bred to talk, man; when we are armed\nAnd both upon our guards, then let our fury,\nLike meeting of two tides, fly strongly from us;\nAnd then to whom the birthright of this beauty\nTruly pertains—without upbraidings, scorns,\nDespisings of our persons, and such poutings,\nFitter for girls and schoolboys—will be seen,\nAnd quickly, yours or mine. Will ’t please you arm, sir?\nOr, if you feel yourself not fitting yet\nAnd furnished with your old strength, I’ll stay, cousin,\nAnd every day discourse you into health,\nAs I am spared. Your person I am friends with,\nAnd I could wish I had not said I loved her,\nThough I had died; but, loving such a lady,\nAnd justifying my love, I must not fly from ’t.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arcite, thou art so brave an enemy,\nThat no man but thy cousin’s fit to kill thee.\nI am well and lusty; choose your arms.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Choose you, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou exceed in all, or dost thou do it\nTo make me spare thee?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you think so, cousin,\nYou are deceived, for as I am a soldier,\nI will not spare you.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s well said.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You’ll find it.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, as I am an honest man and love\nWith all the justice of affection,\nI’ll pay thee soundly.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[He chooses armour.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This I’ll take.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s mine, then.\nI’ll arm you first.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Arcite begins arming him.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray thee, tell me, cousin,\nWhere got’st thou this good armour?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis the Duke’s,\nAnd, to say true, I stole it. Do I pinch you?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is’t not too heavy?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have worn a lighter,\nBut I shall make it serve.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll buckle ’t close.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By any means.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You care not for a grand guard?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no; we’ll use no horses: I perceive\nYou would fain be at that fight.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am indifferent.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, so am I. Good cousin, thrust the buckle\nThrough far enough.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I warrant you.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My casque now.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you fight bare-armed?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We shall be the nimbler.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But use your gauntlets though. Those are o’ th’ least;\nPrithee take mine, good cousin.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thank you, Arcite.\nHow do I look? Am I fall’n much away?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, very little; love has used you kindly.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll warrant thee, I’ll strike home.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, and spare not.\nI’ll give you cause, sweet cousin.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now to you, sir.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[He begins to arm Arcite.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methinks this armour’s very like that, Arcite,\nThou wor’st that day the three kings fell, but lighter.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That was a very good one; and that day,\nI well remember, you outdid me, cousin;\nI never saw such valour. When you charged\nUpon the left wing of the enemy,\nI spurred hard to come up, and under me\nI had a right good horse.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You had indeed;\nA bright bay, I remember.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, but all\nWas vainly laboured in me; you outwent me,\nNor could my wishes reach you. Yet a little\nI did by imitation.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"More by virtue;\nYou are modest, cousin.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When I saw you charge first,\nMe thought I heard a dreadful clap of thunder\nBreak from the troop.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But still before that flew\nThe lightning of your valour. Stay a little;\nIs not this piece too strait?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, ’tis well.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would have nothing hurt thee but my sword.\nA bruise would be dishonour.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now I am perfect.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand off, then.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take my sword; I hold it better.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank ye, no; keep it; your life lies on it.\nHere’s one; if it but hold, I ask no more\nFor all my hopes. My cause and honour guard me!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And me my love!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:59"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They bow several ways, then advance and stand.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is there aught else to say?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This only, and no more. Thou art mine aunt’s son.\nAnd that blood we desire to shed is mutual,\nIn me thine, and in thee mine. My sword\nIs in my hand, and if thou killest me,\nThe gods and I forgive thee. If there be\nA place prepared for those that sleep in honour,\nI wish his weary soul that falls may win it.\nFight bravely, cousin; give me thy noble hand.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, Palamon. This hand shall never more\nCome near thee with such friendship.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I commend thee.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I fall, curse me, and say I was a coward,\nFor none but such dare die in these just trials.\nOnce more farewell, my cousin.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, Arcite.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:66"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They fight. Horns within. They stand.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lo, cousin, lo, our folly has undone us.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the Duke, a-hunting, as I told you.\nIf we be found, we are wretched. O, retire,\nFor honour’s sake and safety, presently\nInto your bush again. Sir, we shall find\nToo many hours to die in. Gentle cousin,\nIf you be seen, you perish instantly\nFor breaking prison and I, if you reveal me,\nFor my contempt. Then all the world will scorn us,\nAnd say we had a noble difference,\nBut base disposers of it.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, cousin,\nI will no more be hidden, nor put off\nThis great adventure to a second trial;\nI know your cunning and I know your cause.\nHe that faints now, shame take him! Put thyself\nUpon thy present guard—","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are not mad?","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or I will make th’advantage of this hour\nMine own, and what to come shall threaten me\nI fear less than my fortune. Know, weak cousin,\nI love Emilia, and in that I’ll bury\nThee, and all crosses else.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, come what can come,\nThou shalt know, Palamon, I dare as well\nDie, as discourse, or sleep. Only this fears me,\nThe law will have the honour of our ends.\nHave at thy life!","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look to thine own well, Arcite.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:75"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They fight. Horns within. They stand.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Emilia, Pirithous and\ntrain.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What ignorant and mad malicious traitors\nAre you, that ’gainst the tenor of my laws\nAre making battle, thus like knights appointed,\nWithout my leave, and officers of arms?\nBy Castor, both shall die.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold thy word, Theseus.\nWe are certainly both traitors, both despisers\nOf thee and of thy goodness. I am Palamon,\nThat cannot love thee, he that broke thy prison.\nThink well what that deserves. And this is Arcite.\nA bolder traitor never trod thy ground,\nA falser ne’er seemed friend. This is the man\nWas begged and banished; this is he contemns thee\nAnd what thou dar’st do; and in this disguise,\nAgainst thine own edict, follows thy sister,\nThat fortunate bright star, the fair Emilia,\nWhose servant—if there be a right in seeing\nAnd first bequeathing of the soul to—justly\nI am; and, which is more, dares think her his.\nThis treachery, like a most trusty lover,\nI called him now to answer. If thou be’st\nAs thou art spoken, great and virtuous,\nThe true decider of all injuries,\nSay “Fight again,” and thou shalt see me, Theseus,\nDo such a justice thou thyself wilt envy.\nThen take my life; I’ll woo thee to ’t.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O heaven,\nWhat more than man is this!","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have sworn.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We seek not\nThy breath of mercy, Theseus. ’Tis to me\nA thing as soon to die as thee to say it,\nAnd no more moved. Where this man calls me traitor,\nLet me say thus much: if in love be treason,\nIn service of so excellent a beauty,\nAs I love most, and in that faith will perish,\nAs I have brought my life here to confirm it,\nAs I have served her truest, worthiest,\nAs I dare kill this cousin that denies it,\nSo let me be most traitor, and you please me.\nFor scorning thy edict, Duke, ask that lady\nWhy she is fair, and why her eyes command me\nStay here to love her; and if she say “traitor,”\nI am a villain fit to lie unburied.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou shalt have pity of us both, O Theseus,\nIf unto neither thou show mercy. Stop,\nAs thou art just, thy noble ear against us;\nAs thou art valiant, for thy cousin’s soul,\nWhose twelve strong labours crown his memory,\nLet’s die together at one instant, Duke;\nOnly a little let him fall before me,\nThat I may tell my soul he shall not have her.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I grant your wish, for, to say true, your cousin\nHas ten times more offended, for I gave him\nMore mercy than you found, sir, your offences\nBeing no more than his. None here speak for ’em,\nFor, ere the sun set, both shall sleep for ever.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas the pity! Now or never, sister,\nSpeak, not to be denied. That face of yours\nWill bear the curses else of after ages\nFor these lost cousins.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In my face, dear sister,\nI find no anger to ’em, nor no ruin;\nThe misadventure of their own eyes kill ’em.\nYet that I will be woman and have pity,\nMy knees shall grow to’ th’ ground but I’ll get mercy.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:86"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[She kneels.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Help me, dear sister; in a deed so virtuous\nThe powers of all women will be with us.\nMost royal brother—","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Kneels.] Sir, by our tie of marriage—","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By your own spotless honour—","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By that faith,\nThat fair hand, and that honest heart you gave me—","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By that you would have pity in another,\nBy your own virtues infinite—","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By valour,\nBy all the chaste nights I have ever pleased you—","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These are strange conjurings.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then, I’ll in too.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:95"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Kneels.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By all our friendship, sir, by all our dangers,\nBy all you love most: wars and this sweet lady—","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By that you would have trembled to deny\nA blushing maid—","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By your own eyes, by strength,\nIn which you swore I went beyond all women,\nAlmost all men, and yet I yielded, Theseus—","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To crown all this, by your most noble soul,\nWhich cannot want due mercy, I beg first.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Next, hear my prayers.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Last, let me entreat, sir.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For mercy.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mercy.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mercy on these princes.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ye make my faith reel. Say I felt\nCompassion to’em both, how would you place it?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:106"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Emilia, Hippolyta and\nPirithous rise.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon their lives. But with their banishments.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are a right woman, sister: you have pity,\nBut want the understanding where to use it.\nIf you desire their lives, invent a way\nSafer than banishment. Can these two live,\nAnd have the agony of love about ’em,\nAnd not kill one another? Every day\nThey’d fight about you, hourly bring your honour\nIn public question with their swords. Be wise, then,\nAnd here forget ’em; it concerns your credit\nAnd my oath equally. I have said they die.\nBetter they fall by th’ law than one another.\nBow not my honour.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my noble brother,\nThat oath was rashly made, and in your anger;\nYour reason will not hold it; if such vows\nStand for express will, all the world must perish.\nBesides, I have another oath ’gainst yours,\nOf more authority, I am sure more love,\nNot made in passion neither, but good heed.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is it, sister?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Urge it home, brave lady.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That you would ne’er deny me anything\nFit for my modest suit and your free granting.\nI tie you to your word now; if ye fail in ’t,\nThink how you maim your honour—\nFor now I am set a-begging, sir, I am deaf\nTo all but your compassion—how their lives\nMight breed the ruin of my name. Opinion!\nShall anything that loves me perish for me?\nThat were a cruel wisdom. Do men prune\nThe straight young boughs that blush with thousand blossoms\nBecause they may be rotten? O, Duke Theseus,\nThe goodly mothers that have groaned for these,\nAnd all the longing maids that ever loved,\nIf your vow stand, shall curse me and my beauty,\nAnd in their funeral songs for these two cousins\nDespise my cruelty, and cry woe worth me,\nTill I am nothing but the scorn of women.\nFor heaven’s sake, save their lives, and banish ’em.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"On what conditions?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Swear ’em never more\nTo make me their contention, or to know me,\nTo tread upon thy dukedom, and to be,\nWherever they shall travel, ever strangers\nTo one another.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll be cut a-pieces\nBefore I take this oath! Forget I love her?\nO, all ye gods, despise me then! Thy banishment\nI not mislike, so we may fairly carry\nOur swords and cause along; else never trifle,\nBut take our lives, Duke. I must love, and will\nAnd for that love must and dare kill this cousin\nOn any piece the earth has.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you, Arcite,\nTake these conditions?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’s a villain, then.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These are men!","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, never, Duke. ’Tis worse to me than begging\nTo take my life so basely. Though I think\nI never shall enjoy her, yet I’ll preserve\nThe honour of affection, and die for her,\nMake death a devil.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What may be done? For now I feel compassion.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let it not fall again, sir.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, Emilia,\nIf one of them were dead, as one must, are you\nContent to take th’ other to your husband?\nThey cannot both enjoy you. They are princes\nAs goodly as your own eyes, and as noble\nAs ever fame yet spoke of. Look upon ’em,\nAnd, if you can love, end this difference;\nI give consent.—Are you content too, princes?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With all our souls.","speaker":"BOTH","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:124"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He that she refuses\nMust die, then.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Any death thou canst invent, Duke.","speaker":"BOTH","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:126"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I fall from that mouth, I fall with favour,\nAnd lovers yet unborn shall bless my ashes.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:127"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If she refuse me, yet my grave will wed me,\nAnd soldiers sing my epitaph.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:128"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make choice, then.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:129"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot, sir, they are both too excellent;\nFor me, a hair shall never fall of these men.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:130"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What will become of ’em?","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:131"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus I ordain it\nAnd, by mine honour, once again, it stands,\nOr both shall die. You shall both to your country,\nAnd each within this month, accompanied\nWith three fair knights, appear again in this place,\nIn which I’ll plant a pyramid; and whether,\nBefore us that are here, can force his cousin\nBy fair and knightly strength to touch the pillar,\nHe shall enjoy her; th’ other lose his head,\nAnd all his friends; nor shall he grudge to fall,\nNor think he dies with interest in this lady.\nWill this content ye?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:132"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes. Here, cousin Arcite,\nI am friends again, till that hour.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:133"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[He offers his hand.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:134"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I embrace ye.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:135"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you content, sister?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:136"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, I must, sir,\nElse both miscarry.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:137"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, shake hands again, then;\nAnd take heed, as you are gentlemen, this quarrel\nSleep till the hour prefixed, and hold your course.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:138"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We dare not fail thee, Theseus.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:139"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They shake hands.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:140"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, I’ll give ye\nNow usage like to princes, and to friends.\nWhen ye return, who wins, I’ll settle here;\nWho loses, yet I’ll weep upon his bier.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:141"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:3.6:142"}],"scene":6,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"4.1","label":"Act 4 · SCENE I. Athens. A room in the prison","act":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer and his\nFriend.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear you no more? Was nothing said of me\nConcerning the escape of Palamon?\nGood sir, remember.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing that I heard,\nFor I came home before the business\nWas fully ended. Yet I might perceive,\nEre I departed, a great likelihood\nOf both their pardons; for Hippolyta\nAnd fair-eyed Emily, upon their knees,\nBegged with such handsome pity that the Duke\nMethought stood staggering whether he should follow\nHis rash oath or the sweet compassion\nOf those two ladies. And, to second them,\nThat truly noble prince, Pirithous,\nHalf his own heart, set in too, that I hope\nAll shall be well. Neither heard I one question\nOf your name or his ’scape.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray heaven it hold so.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Second Friend.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be of good comfort, man; I bring you news,\nGood news.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are welcome.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Palamon has cleared you,\nAnd got your pardon, and discovered how\nAnd by whose means he escaped, which was your daughter’s,\nWhose pardon is procured too; and the prisoner,\nNot to be held ungrateful to her goodness,\nHas given a sum of money to her marriage,\nA large one, I’ll assure you.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are a good man\nAnd ever bring good news.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How was it ended?","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, as it should be; they that never begged\nBut they prevailed had their suits fairly granted;\nThe prisoners have their lives.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I knew ’twould be so.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But there be new conditions, which you’ll hear of\nAt better time.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope they are good.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are honourable;\nHow good they’ll prove, I know not.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Twill be known.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Wooer.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, sir, where’s your daughter?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you ask?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, sir, when did you see her?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How he looks?","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This morning.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was she well? Was she in health, sir?\nWhen did she sleep?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These are strange questions.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not think she was very well, for now\nYou make me mind her, but this very day\nI asked her questions, and she answered me\nSo far from what she was, so childishly,\nSo sillily, as if she were a fool,\nAn innocent, and I was very angry.\nBut what of her, sir?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing but my pity.\nBut you must know it, and as good by me\nAs by another that less loves her.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not right?","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not well?","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, sir, not well:\n’Tis too true, she is mad.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It cannot be.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Believe, you’ll find it so.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I half suspected\nWhat you have told me. The gods comfort her!\nEither this was her love to Palamon,\nOr fear of my miscarrying on his ’scape,\nOr both.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis likely.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But why all this haste, sir?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll tell you quickly. As I late was angling\nIn the great lake that lies behind the palace,\nFrom the far shore, thick set with reeds and sedges,\nAs patiently I was attending sport,\nI heard a voice, a shrill one; and, attentive,\nI gave my ear, when I might well perceive\n’Twas one that sung, and by the smallness of it\nA boy or woman. I then left my angle\nTo his own skill, came near, but yet perceived not\nWho made the sound, the rushes and the reeds\nHad so encompassed it. I laid me down\nAnd listened to the words she sung, for then,\nThrough a small glade cut by the fishermen,\nI saw it was your daughter.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, go on, sir.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She sung much, but no sense; only I heard her\nRepeat this often: “Palamon is gone,\nIs gone to th’ wood to gather mulberries;\nI’ll find him out tomorrow.”","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pretty soul!","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"“His shackles will betray him; he’ll be taken,\nAnd what shall I do then? I’ll bring a bevy,\nA hundred black-eyed maids that love as I do,\nWith chaplets on their heads of daffadillies,\nWith cherry lips and cheeks of damask roses,\nAnd all we’ll dance an antic ’fore the Duke,\nAnd beg his pardon.” Then she talked of you, sir;\nThat you must lose your head tomorrow morning,\nAnd she must gather flowers to bury you,\nAnd see the house made handsome. Then she sung\nNothing but “Willow, willow, willow,” and between\nEver was “Palamon, fair Palamon,”\nAnd “Palamon was a tall young man.” The place\nWas knee-deep where she sat; her careless tresses,\nA wreath of bulrush rounded; about her stuck\nThousand fresh water-flowers of several colours,\nThat methought she appeared like the fair nymph\nThat feeds the lake with waters, or as Iris\nNewly dropped down from heaven. Rings she made\nOf rushes that grew by, and to ’em spoke\nThe prettiest posies: “Thus our true love’s tied,”\n“This you may loose, not me,” and many a one;\nAnd then she wept, and sung again, and sighed,\nAnd with the same breath smiled and kissed her hand.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, what pity it is!","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I made in to her.\nShe saw me, and straight sought the flood. I saved her\nAnd set her safe to land, when presently\nShe slipped away, and to the city made\nWith such a cry and swiftness that, believe me,\nShe left me far behind her. Three or four\nI saw from far off cross her—one of ’em\nI knew to be your brother—where she stayed\nAnd fell, scarce to be got away. I left them with her\nAnd hither came to tell you.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Brother, Jailer’s Daughter and\nothers.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here they are.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Sings.]","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May you never more enjoy the light, &c.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is not this a fine song?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, a very fine one.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I can sing twenty more.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think you can.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, truly can I. I can sing “The Broom”\nand “Bonny Robin.” Are not you a tailor?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where’s my wedding gown?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll bring it tomorrow.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, very rarely, I must be abroad else\nTo call the maids and pay the minstrels,\nFor I must lose my maidenhead by cocklight.\n’Twill never thrive else.\n[Sings.] O fair, O sweet, &c.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To Jailer.] You must e’en take it patiently.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis true.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good ev’n, good men; pray, did you ever hear\nOf one young Palamon?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, wench, we know him.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is’t not a fine young gentleman?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis, love.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By no means cross her; she is then distempered\nFar worse than now she shows.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, he’s a fine man.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, is he so? You have a sister?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But she shall never have him, tell her so,\nFor a trick that I know; you’d best look to her,\nFor if she see him once, she’s gone, she’s done,\nAnd undone in an hour. All the young maids\nOf our town are in love with him, but I laugh at ’em\nAnd let ’em all alone. Is ’t not a wise course?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is at least two hundred now with child by him—\nThere must be four; yet I keep close for all this,\nClose as a cockle; and all these must be boys\nHe has the trick on ’t; and at ten years old\nThey must be all gelt for musicians\nAnd sing the wars of Theseus.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is strange.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As ever you heard, but say nothing.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They come from all parts of the dukedom to him.\nI’ll warrant ye, he had not so few last night\nAs twenty to dispatch. He’ll tickle ’t up\nIn two hours, if his hand be in.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She’s lost\nPast all cure.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Heaven forbid, man!","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, you are a wise man.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside.] Does she know him?","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside.] No, would she did.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are master of a ship?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where’s your compass?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Set it to th’ north.\nAnd now direct your course to th’ wood, where Palamon\nLies longing for me. For the tackling,\nLet me alone. Come, weigh, my hearts, cheerly.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Owgh, owgh, owgh! ’Tis up, the wind’s fair!\nTop the bowline; out with the mainsail;\nWhere’s your whistle, master?","speaker":"ALL","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let’s get her in.","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Up to the top, boy.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where’s the pilot?","speaker":"BROTHER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here.","speaker":"FIRST FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What kenn’st thou?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A fair wood.","speaker":"SECOND FRIEND","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear for it, master. Tack about!\n[Sings.]\nWhen Cinthia with her borrowed light, &c.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:90"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.1:91"}],"scene":1},{"id":"4.2","label":"Act 4 · SCENE II. A Room in the Palace","act":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Emilia alone, with two pictures.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet I may bind those wounds up, that must open\nAnd bleed to death for my sake else. I’ll choose,\nAnd end their strife. Two such young handsome men\nShall never fall for me; their weeping mothers,\nFollowing the dead cold ashes of their sons,\nShall never curse my cruelty.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Looks at one of the pictures.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good heaven,\nWhat a sweet face has Arcite! If wise Nature,\nWith all her best endowments, all those beauties\nShe sows into the births of noble bodies,\nWere here a mortal woman, and had in her\nThe coy denials of young maids, yet doubtless\nShe would run mad for this man. What an eye,\nOf what a fiery sparkle and quick sweetness,\nHas this young prince! Here Love himself sits smiling;\nJust such another wanton Ganymede\nSet Jove afire with, and enforced the god\nSnatch up the goodly boy and set him by him,\nA shining constellation. What a brow,\nOf what a spacious majesty, he carries,\nArched like the great-eyed Juno’s, but far sweeter,\nSmoother than Pelops’ shoulder! Fame and Honour,\nMethinks, from hence, as from a promontory\nPointed in heaven, should clap their wings and sing\nTo all the under-world the loves and fights\nOf gods and such men near ’em.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Looks at the other picture.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Palamon\nIs but his foil; to him a mere dull shadow;\nHe’s swart and meagre, of an eye as heavy\nAs if he had lost his mother; a still temper,\nNo stirring in him, no alacrity;\nOf all this sprightly sharpness, not a smile.\nYet these that we count errors may become him;\nNarcissus was a sad boy but a heavenly.\nO, who can find the bent of woman’s fancy?\nI am a fool; my reason is lost in me;\nI have no choice, and I have lied so lewdly\nThat women ought to beat me. On my knees\nI ask thy pardon, Palamon, thou art alone\nAnd only beautiful, and these the eyes,\nThese the bright lamps of beauty, that command\nAnd threaten love, and what young maid dare cross ’em?\nWhat a bold gravity, and yet inviting,\nHas this brown manly face! O Love, this only\nFrom this hour is complexion. Lie there, Arcite.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[She puts aside his picture.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou art a changeling to him, a mere gypsy,\nAnd this the noble body. I am sotted,\nUtterly lost. My virgin’s faith has fled me.\nFor if my brother but even now had asked me\nWhether I loved, I had run mad for Arcite;\nNow, if my sister, more for Palamon.\nStand both together. Now, come ask me, brother.\nAlas, I know not! Ask me now, sweet sister.\nI may go look! What a mere child is Fancy,\nThat, having two fair gauds of equal sweetness,\nCannot distinguish, but must cry for both.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Gentleman.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, sir?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From the noble Duke your brother,\nMadam, I bring you news. The knights are come.","speaker":"GENTLEMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To end the quarrel?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"GENTLEMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would I might end first!\nWhat sins have I committed, chaste Diana,\nThat my unspotted youth must now be soiled\nWith blood of princes, and my chastity\nBe made the altar where the lives of lovers—\nTwo greater and two better never yet\nMade mothers joy—must be the sacrifice\nTo my unhappy beauty?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Pirithous and Attendants.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring ’em in\nQuickly, by any means; I long to see ’em.\nYour two contending lovers are returned,\nAnd with them their fair knights. Now, my fair sister,\nYou must love one of them.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had rather both,\nSo neither for my sake should fall untimely.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who saw ’em?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I a while.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I.","speaker":"GENTLEMAN","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Messenger.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From whence come you, sir?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From the knights.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, speak,\nYou that have seen them, what they are.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, sir,\nAnd truly what I think. Six braver spirits\nThan these they have brought, if we judge by the outside,\nI never saw nor read of. He that stands\nIn the first place with Arcite, by his seeming\nShould be a stout man, by his face a prince,\nHis very looks so say him; his complexion\nNearer a brown than black, stern and yet noble,\nWhich shows him hardy, fearless, proud of dangers;\nThe circles of his eyes show fire within him,\nAnd as a heated lion so he looks.\nHis hair hangs long behind him, black and shining\nLike ravens’ wings; his shoulders broad and strong;\nArmed long and round; and on his thigh a sword\nHung by a curious baldric, when he frowns\nTo seal his will with. Better, o’ my conscience,\nWas never soldier’s friend.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast well described him.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet a great deal short,\nMethinks, of him that’s first with Palamon.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, speak him, friend.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I guess he is a prince too,\nAnd, if it may be, greater; for his show\nHas all the ornament of honour in ’t:\nHe’s somewhat bigger than the knight he spoke of,\nBut of a face far sweeter; his complexion\nIs, as a ripe grape, ruddy. He has felt\nWithout doubt what he fights for, and so apter\nTo make this cause his own. In ’s face appears\nAll the fair hopes of what he undertakes\nAnd when he’s angry, then a settled valour,\nNot tainted with extremes, runs through his body\nAnd guides his arm to brave things. Fear he cannot;\nHe shows no such soft temper. His head’s yellow,\nHard-haired and curled, thick-twined like ivy tods,\nNot to undo with thunder. In his face\nThe livery of the warlike maid appears,\nPure red and white, for yet no beard has blessed him;\nAnd in his rolling eyes sits Victory,\nAs if she ever meant to crown his valour.\nHis nose stands high, a character of honour;\nHis red lips, after fights, are fit for ladies.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Must these men die too?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When he speaks, his tongue\nSounds like a trumpet. All his lineaments\nAre as a man would wish ’em, strong and clean.\nHe wears a well-steeled axe, the staff of gold;\nHis age some five-and-twenty.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There’s another,\nA little man, but of a tough soul, seeming\nAs great as any; fairer promises\nIn such a body yet I never looked on.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, he that’s freckle-faced?","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The same, my lord;\nAre they not sweet ones?","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, they are well.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methinks,\nBeing so few and well disposed, they show\nGreat and fine art in nature. He’s white-haired,\nNot wanton white, but such a manly colour\nNext to an auburn; tough and nimble-set,\nWhich shows an active soul. His arms are brawny,\nLined with strong sinews. To the shoulder-piece\nGently they swell, like women new-conceived,\nWhich speaks him prone to labour, never fainting\nUnder the weight of arms; stout-hearted still,\nBut when he stirs, a tiger. He’s grey-eyed,\nWhich yields compassion where he conquers; sharp\nTo spy advantages, and where he finds ’em,\nHe’s swift to make ’em his. He does no wrongs,\nNor takes none. He’s round-faced, and when he smiles\nHe shows a lover; when he frowns, a soldier.\nAbout his head he wears the winner’s oak,\nAnd in it stuck the favour of his lady.\nHis age some six-and-thirty. In his hand\nHe bears a charging-staff embossed with silver.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are they all thus?","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are all the sons of honour.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, as I have a soul, I long to see’em.\nLady, you shall see men fight now.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I wish it,\nBut not the cause, my lord. They would show\nBravely about the titles of two kingdoms.\n’Tis pity love should be so tyrannous.—\nO, my soft-hearted sister, what think you?\nWeep not till they weep blood. Wench, it must be.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have steeled ’em with your beauty.\nHonoured friend,\nTo you I give the field; pray order it\nFitting the persons that must use it.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, sir.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, I’ll go visit ’em. I cannot stay,\nTheir fame has fired me so; till they appear.\nGood friend, be royal.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There shall want no bravery.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt all but Emilia.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor wench, go weep, for whosoever wins,\nLoses a noble cousin for thy sins.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.2:47"}],"scene":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"4.3","label":"Act 4 · SCENE III. A room in the prison","act":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer, Wooer and\nDoctor.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her distraction is more at some time of the moon, than at other\nsome, is it not?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is continually in a harmless distemper, sleeps little, altogether without\nappetite, save often drinking, dreaming of another world, and a better; and\nwhat broken piece of matter soe’er she’s about, the name Palamon lards it, that\nshe farces every business withal, fits it to every question.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer’s Daughter.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look where she comes; you shall perceive her behaviour.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have forgot it quite. The burden on ’t was “Down-a, down-a,” and penned by no\nworse man than Geraldo, Emilia’s schoolmaster. He’s as fantastical, too, as\never he may go upon’s legs, for in the next world will Dido see Palamon, and\nthen will she be out of love with Æneas.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stuff’s here? Poor soul!","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even thus all day long.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now for this charm that I told you of: you must bring a piece of silver on the\ntip of your tongue, or no ferry. Then if it be your chance to come where the\nblessed spirits are, there’s a sight now! We maids that have our livers\nperished, cracked to pieces with love, we shall come there, and do nothing all\nday long but pick flowers with Proserpine. Then will I make Palamon a nosegay;\nthen let him mark me—then.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How prettily she’s amiss! Note her a little further.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, I’ll tell you, sometime we go to barley-break, we of the blessed. Alas,\n’tis a sore life they have i’ th’ other place—such burning, frying, boiling,\nhissing, howling, chattering, cursing—O, they have shrewd measure; take heed!\nIf one be mad, or hang or drown themselves, thither they go; Jupiter bless us!\nAnd there shall we be put in a cauldron of lead and usurers’ grease, amongst a\nwhole million of cutpurses, and there boil like a gammon of bacon that will\nnever be enough.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How her brain coins!","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lords and courtiers that have got maids with child, they are in this place.\nThey shall stand in fire up to the navel and in ice up to the heart, and there\nth’ offending part burns and the deceiving part freezes. In troth, a very\ngrievous punishment, as one would think, for such a trifle. Believe me, one\nwould marry a leprous witch to be rid on ’t, I’ll assure you.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How she continues this fancy! ’Tis not an engraffed madness, but a most thick,\nand profound melancholy.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To hear there a proud lady and a proud city wife howl together! I were a beast\nan I’d call it good sport. One cries “O this smoke!” th’ other, “This fire!”;\none cries, “O, that ever I did it behind the arras!” and then howls; th’ other\ncurses a suing fellow and her garden house.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Sings.]\nI will be true, my stars, my fate, &c.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Jailer’s Daughter.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What think you of her, sir?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think she has a perturbed mind, which I cannot minister to.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, what then?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Understand you she ever affected any man ere she beheld Palamon?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I was once, sir, in great hope she had fixed her liking on this gentleman, my\nfriend.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did think so too, and would account I had a great penn’orth on’t, to give\nhalf my state, that both she and I at this present stood unfeignedly on the\nsame terms.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That intemperate surfeit of her eye hath distempered the other senses. They may\nreturn and settle again to execute their preordained faculties, but they are\nnow in a most extravagant vagary. This you must do: confine her to a place\nwhere the light may rather seem to steal in than be permitted. Take upon you,\nyoung sir, her friend, the name of Palamon; say you come to eat with her, and\nto commune of love. This will catch her attention, for this her mind beats\nupon; other objects that are inserted ’tween her mind and eye become the pranks\nand friskins of her madness. Sing to her such green songs of love as she says\nPalamon hath sung in prison. Come to her stuck in as sweet flowers as the\nseason is mistress of, and thereto make an addition of some other compounded\nodours which are grateful to the sense. All this shall become Palamon, for\nPalamon can sing, and Palamon is sweet and every good thing. Desire to eat with\nher, carve her, drink to her, and still among intermingle your petition of\ngrace and acceptance into her favour. Learn what maids have been her companions\nand play-feres, and let them repair to her with Palamon in their mouths, and\nappear with tokens, as if they suggested for him. It is a falsehood she is in,\nwhich is with falsehoods to be combated. This may bring her to eat, to sleep,\nand reduce what’s now out of square in her into their former law and regiment.\nI have seen it approved, how many times I know not, but to make the number\nmore I have great hope in this. I will, between the passages of this project,\ncome in with my appliance. Let us put it in execution and hasten the success,\nwhich, doubt not, will bring forth comfort.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:4.3:25"}],"scene":3,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"5.1","label":"Act 5 · SCENE I. Athens. Before the Temples of Mars, Venus, and Diana","act":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter Theseus, Pirithous, Hippolyta and\nAttendants.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now let ’em enter and before the gods\nTender their holy prayers. Let the temples\nBurn bright with sacred fires, and the altars\nIn hallowed clouds commend their swelling incense\nTo those above us. Let no due be wanting.\nThey have a noble work in hand, will honour\nThe very powers that love ’em.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, they enter.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and\nArcite and their Knights.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You valiant and strong-hearted enemies,\nYou royal german foes, that this day come\nTo blow that nearness out that flames between ye,\nLay by your anger for an hour and, dove-like,\nBefore the holy altars of your helpers,\nThe all-feared gods, bow down your stubborn bodies.\nYour ire is more than mortal; so your help be;\nAnd, as the gods regard ye, fight with justice.\nI’ll leave you to your prayers, and betwixt ye\nI part my wishes.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Honour crown the worthiest.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Theseus and his Train.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The glass is running now that cannot finish\nTill one of us expire. Think you but thus,\nThat were there aught in me which strove to show\nMine enemy in this business, were ’t one eye\nAgainst another, arm oppressed by arm,\nI would destroy th’ offender, coz, I would\nThough parcel of myself. Then from this gather\nHow I should tender you.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am in labour\nTo push your name, your ancient love, our kindred\nOut of my memory, and i’ th’ selfsame place\nTo seat something I would confound. So hoist we\nThe sails that must these vessels port even where\nThe heavenly limiter pleases.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You speak well.\nBefore I turn, let me embrace thee, cousin.\nThis I shall never do again.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One farewell.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, let it be so. Farewell, coz.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, sir.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt Palamon and his Knights.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Knights, kinsmen, lovers, yea, my sacrifices,\nTrue worshippers of Mars, whose spirit in you\nExpels the seeds of fear and th’ apprehension\nWhich still is father of it, go with me\nBefore the god of our profession. There\nRequire of him the hearts of lions and\nThe breath of tigers, yea, the fierceness too,\nYea, the speed also—to go on, I mean;\nElse wish we to be snails. You know my prize\nMust be dragged out of blood; force and great feat\nMust put my garland on, where she sticks,\nThe queen of flowers. Our intercession, then,\nMust be to him that makes the camp a cistern\nBrimmed with the blood of men. Give me your aid,\nAnd bend your spirits towards him.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They advance to the altar of Mars, fall on their faces before it, and then kneel.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou mighty one, that with thy power hast turned\nGreen Neptune into purple; whose approach\nComets prewarn, whose havoc in vast field\nUnearthed skulls proclaim; whose breath blows down\nThe teeming Ceres’ foison, who dost pluck\nWith hand armipotent from forth blue clouds\nThe masoned turrets, that both mak’st and break’st\nThe stony girths of cities; me thy pupil,\nYoungest follower of thy drum, instruct this day\nWith military skill, that to thy laud\nI may advance my streamer, and by thee\nBe styled the lord o’ th’ day. Give me, great Mars,\nSome token of thy pleasure.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Here they fall on their faces as formerly, and there is\nheard clanging of armour, with a short thunder, as the burst of a battle,\nwhereupon they all rise and bow to the altar.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, great corrector of enormous times,\nShaker of o’er-rank states, thou grand decider\nOf dusty and old titles, that heal’st with blood\nThe earth when it is sick, and cur’st the world\nO’ th’ pleurisy of people; I do take\nThy signs auspiciously, and in thy name\nTo my design march boldly.—Let us go.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and his Knights, with the former\nobservance.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our stars must glister with new fire, or be\nToday extinct. Our argument is love,\nWhich, if the goddess of it grant, she gives\nVictory too. Then blend your spirits with mine,\nYou whose free nobleness do make my cause\nYour personal hazard. To the goddess Venus\nCommend we our proceeding, and implore\nHer power unto our party.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Here they kneel as formerly.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hail, sovereign queen of secrets, who hast power\nTo call the fiercest tyrant from his rage\nAnd weep unto a girl; that hast the might\nEven with an eye-glance to choke Mars’s drum\nAnd turn th’ alarm to whispers; that canst make\nA cripple flourish with his crutch, and cure him\nBefore Apollo; that mayst force the king\nTo be his subject’s vassal, and induce\nStale gravity to dance. The polled bachelor,\nWhose youth, like wanton boys through bonfires,\nHave skipped thy flame, at seventy thou canst catch,\nAnd make him, to the scorn of his hoarse throat,\nAbuse young lays of love. What godlike power\nHast thou not power upon? To Phœbus thou\nAdd’st flames hotter than his; the heavenly fires\nDid scorch his mortal son, thine him. The huntress,\nAll moist and cold, some say, began to throw\nHer bow away and sigh. Take to thy grace\nMe, thy vowed soldier, who do bear thy yoke\nAs ’twere a wreath of roses, yet is heavier\nThan lead itself, stings more than nettles.\nI have never been foul-mouthed against thy law,\nNe’er revealed secret, for I knew none—would not,\nHad I kenned all that were. I never practised\nUpon man’s wife, nor would the libels read\nOf liberal wits. I never at great feasts\nSought to betray a beauty, but have blushed\nAt simpering sirs that did. I have been harsh\nTo large confessors, and have hotly asked them\nIf they had mothers—I had one, a woman,\nAnd women ’twere they wronged. I knew a man\nOf eighty winters, this I told them, who\nA lass of fourteen brided; ’twas thy power\nTo put life into dust. The aged cramp\nHad screwed his square foot round;\nThe gout had knit his fingers into knots,\nTorturing convulsions from his globy eyes\nHad almost drawn their spheres, that what was life\nIn him seemed torture. This anatomy\nHad by his young fair fere a boy, and I\nBelieved it was his, for she swore it was,\nAnd who would not believe her? Brief, I am\nTo those that prate and have done, no companion;\nTo those that boast and have not, a defier;\nTo those that would and cannot, a rejoicer.\nYea, him I do not love that tells close offices\nThe foulest way, nor names concealments in\nThe boldest language. Such a one I am,\nAnd vow that lover never yet made sigh\nTruer than I. O, then, most soft sweet goddess,\nGive me the victory of this question, which\nIs true love’s merit, and bless me with a sign\nOf thy great pleasure.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Here music is heard; doves are seen to flutter. They fall\nagain upon their faces, then on their knees.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O thou that from eleven to ninety reign’st\nIn mortal bosoms, whose chase is this world\nAnd we in herds thy game, I give thee thanks\nFor this fair token, which being laid unto\nMine innocent true heart, arms in assurance\nMy body to this business.—Let us rise\nAnd bow before the goddess.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They rise and bow.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Time comes on.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Still music of recorders. Enter Emilia in white,\nher hair about her shoulders, wearing a wheaten wreath. One in white holding up\nher train, her hair stuck with flowers. One before her carrying a silver hind,\nin which is conveyed incense and sweet odours, which being set upon the altar\nof Diana, her maids standing aloof, she sets\nfire to it; then they curtsy and kneel.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sacred, shadowy, cold, and constant queen,\nAbandoner of revels, mute contemplative,\nSweet, solitary, white as chaste, and pure\nAs wind-fanned snow, who to thy female knights\nAllow’st no more blood than will make a blush,\nWhich is their order’s robe, I here, thy priest,\nAm humbled ’fore thine altar. O, vouchsafe\nWith that thy rare green eye, which never yet\nBeheld thing maculate, look on thy virgin;\nAnd, sacred silver mistress, lend thine ear,\nWhich ne’er heard scurrile term, into whose port\nNe’er entered wanton sound, to my petition,\nSeasoned with holy fear. This is my last\nOf vestal office. I am bride-habited\nBut maiden-hearted. A husband I have ’pointed,\nBut do not know him. Out of two I should\nChoose one, and pray for his success, but I\nAm guiltless of election. Of mine eyes,\nWere I to lose one, they are equal precious;\nI could doom neither; that which perished should\nGo to ’t unsentenced. Therefore, most modest queen,\nHe of the two pretenders that best loves me\nAnd has the truest title in ’t, let him\nTake off my wheaten garland, or else grant\nThe file and quality I hold I may\nContinue in thy band.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Here the hind vanishes under the altar, and in the place\nascends a rose tree, having one rose upon it.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See what our general of ebbs and flows\nOut from the bowels of her holy altar\nWith sacred act advances: but one rose!\nIf well inspired, this battle shall confound\nBoth these brave knights, and I, a virgin flower,\nMust grow alone, unplucked.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Here is heard a sudden twang of instruments, and the rose\nfalls from the tree.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The flower is fall’n, the tree descends. O mistress,\nThou here dischargest me. I shall be gathered;\nI think so, but I know not thine own will.\nUnclasp thy mystery!—I hope she’s pleased;\nHer signs were gracious.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They curtsy and exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.1:36"}],"scene":1},{"id":"5.2","label":"Act 5 · SCENE II. Athens. A Room in the Prison","act":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Doctor, Jailer and Wooer in the habit of\nPalamon.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Has this advice I told you, done any good upon her?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, very much. The maids that kept her company\nHave half persuaded her that I am Palamon;\nWithin this half-hour she came smiling to me,\nAnd asked me what I would eat, and when I would kiss her.\nI told her “Presently,” and kissed her twice.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Twas well done. Twenty times had been far better,\nFor there the cure lies mainly.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then she told me\nShe would watch with me tonight, for well she knew\nWhat hour my fit would take me.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let her do so,\nAnd when your fit comes, fit her home, and presently.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She would have me sing.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You did so?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Twas very ill done, then;\nYou should observe her every way.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas,\nI have no voice, sir, to confirm her that way.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s all one, if ye make a noise.\nIf she entreat again, do anything.\nLie with her, if she ask you.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hoa, there, doctor!","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, in the way of cure.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But first, by your leave,\nI’ th’ way of honesty.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s but a niceness,\nNe’er cast your child away for honesty.\nCure her first this way; then if she will be honest,\nShe has the path before her.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thank ye, Doctor.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, bring her in,\nAnd let’s see how she is.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, and tell her\nHer Palamon stays for her. But, Doctor,\nMethinks you are i’ th’ wrong still.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Jailer.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, go;\nYou fathers are fine fools. Her honesty?\nAn we should give her physic till we find that!","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, do you think she is not honest, sir?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How old is she?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She’s eighteen.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She may be,\nBut that’s all one; ’tis nothing to our purpose.\nWhate’er her father says, if you perceive\nHer mood inclining that way that I spoke of,\nVidelicet, the way of flesh—you have me?","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, very well, sir.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Please her appetite,\nAnd do it home; it cures her, ipso facto,\nThe melancholy humour that infects her.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am of your mind, Doctor.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Jailer, Jailer’s Daughter and\nMaid.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You’ll find it so. She comes, pray, humour her.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, your love Palamon stays for you, child,\nAnd has done this long hour, to visit you.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank him for his gentle patience;\nHe’s a kind gentleman, and I am much bound to him.\nDid you ne’er see the horse he gave me?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How do you like him?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’s a very fair one.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You never saw him dance?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have often.\nHe dances very finely, very comely,\nAnd for a jig, come cut and long tail to him,\nHe turns ye like a top.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s fine, indeed.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He’ll dance the morris twenty mile an hour,\nAnd that will founder the best hobby-horse\nIf I have any skill in all the parish,\nAnd gallops to the tune of “Light o’ love.”\nWhat think you of this horse?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Having these virtues,\nI think he might be brought to play at tennis.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, that’s nothing.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Can he write and read too?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A very fair hand, and casts himself th’ accounts\nOf all his hay and provender. That hostler\nMust rise betime that cozens him. You know\nThe chestnut mare the Duke has?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Very well.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is horribly in love with him, poor beast;\nBut he is like his master, coy and scornful.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What dowry has she?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some two hundred bottles,\nAnd twenty strike of oates; but he’ll ne’er have her.\nHe lisps in’s neighing, able to entice\nA miller’s mare. He’ll be the death of her.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stuff she utters!","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make curtsy; here your love comes.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Wooer and\nDoctor come forward.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pretty soul,\nHow do ye? That’s a fine maid; there’s a curtsy!","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yours to command i’ th’ way of honesty.\nHow far is’t now to’ th’ end o’ th’ world, my masters?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, a day’s journey, wench.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you go with me?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What shall we do there, wench?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, play at stool-ball;\nWhat is there else to do?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am content,\nIf we shall keep our wedding there.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis true,\nFor there, I will assure you, we shall find\nSome blind priest for the purpose, that will venture\nTo marry us, for here they are nice and foolish.\nBesides, my father must be hanged tomorrow,\nAnd that would be a blot i’ th’ business.\nAre not you Palamon?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not you know me?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, but you care not for me. I have nothing\nBut this poor petticoat, and two coarse smocks.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That’s all one; I will have you.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you surely?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Taking her hand.] Yes, by this fair hand, will I.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We’ll to bed, then.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E’en when you will.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:66"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Kisses her.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Rubs off the kiss.] O sir, you would fain be nibbling.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you rub my kiss off?","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis a sweet one,\nAnd will perfume me finely against the wedding.\nIs not this your cousin Arcite?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:70"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[She indicates the Doctor.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, sweetheart,\nAnd I am glad my cousin Palamon\nHas made so fair a choice.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you think he’ll have me?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, without doubt.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you think so too?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We shall have many children. [To Doctor.] Lord, how you’re grown!\nMy Palamon, I hope, will grow too, finely,\nNow he’s at liberty. Alas, poor chicken,\nHe was kept down with hard meat and ill lodging,\nBut I’ll kiss him up again.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:77"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What do you here? You’ll lose the noblest sight\nThat e’er was seen.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are they i’ th’ field?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are.\nYou bear a charge there too.","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll away straight.\nI must e’en leave you here.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, we’ll go with you;\nI will not lose the sight.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How did you like her?","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll warrant you, within these three or four days\nI’ll make her right again. You must not from her,\nBut still preserve her in this way.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let’s get her in.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, sweet, we’ll go to dinner;\nAnd then we’ll play at cards.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And shall we kiss too?","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A hundred times.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And twenty.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, and twenty.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And then we’ll sleep together.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take her offer.","speaker":"DOCTOR","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, marry, will we.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But you shall not hurt me.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will not, sweet.","speaker":"WOOER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you do, love, I’ll cry.","speaker":"DAUGHTER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:98"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.2:99"}],"scene":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"5.3","label":"Act 5 · SCENE III. A part of the Forest near Athens, and near the Place appointed for the Combat","act":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Emilia, Pirithous\nand some Attendants.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll no step further.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you lose this sight?","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had rather see a wren hawk at a fly\nThan this decision. Every blow that falls\nThreats a brave life; each stroke laments\nThe place whereon it falls, and sounds more like\nA bell than blade. I will stay here.\nIt is enough my hearing shall be punished\nWith what shall happen, ’gainst the which there is\nNo deafing, but to hear; not taint mine eye\nWith dread sights it may shun.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, my good lord,\nYour sister will no further.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, she must.\nShe shall see deeds of honour in their kind,\nWhich sometime show well, penciled. Nature now\nShall make and act the story, the belief\nBoth sealed with eye and ear. You must be present;\nYou are the victor’s meed, the price and garland\nTo crown the question’s title.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon me;\nIf I were there, I’d wink.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You must be there;\nThis trial is as ’twere i’ th’ night, and you\nThe only star to shine.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am extinct.\nThere is but envy in that light which shows\nThe one the other. Darkness, which ever was\nThe dam of horror, who does stand accursed\nOf many mortal millions, may even now,\nBy casting her black mantle over both,\nThat neither could find other, get herself\nSome part of a good name, and many a murder\nSet off whereto she’s guilty.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You must go.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In faith, I will not.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, the knights must kindle\nTheir valour at your eye. Know, of this war\nYou are the treasure, and must needs be by\nTo give the service pay.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, pardon me;\nThe title of a kingdom may be tried\nOut of itself.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, well, then, at your pleasure.\nThose that remain with you could wish their office\nTo any of their enemies.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, sister.\nI am like to know your husband ’fore yourself\nBy some small start of time. He whom the gods\nDo of the two know best, I pray them he\nBe made your lot.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt all but Emilia.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arcite is gently visaged, yet his eye\nIs like an engine bent, or a sharp weapon\nIn a soft sheath; mercy and manly courage\nAre bedfellows in his visage. Palamon\nHas a most menacing aspect; his brow\nIs graved, and seems to bury what it frowns on;\nYet sometimes ’tis not so, but alters to\nThe quality of his thoughts. Long time his eye\nWill dwell upon his object. Melancholy\nBecomes him nobly; so does Arcite’s mirth;\nBut Palamon’s sadness is a kind of mirth,\nSo mingled as if mirth did make him sad\nAnd sadness merry. Those darker humours that\nStick misbecomingly on others, on them\nLive in fair dwelling.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Cornets. Trumpets sound as to a charge.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark how yon spurs to spirit do incite\nThe princes to their proof! Arcite may win me\nAnd yet may Palamon wound Arcite to\nThe spoiling of his figure. O, what pity\nEnough for such a chance? If I were by,\nI might do hurt, for they would glance their eyes\nTowards my seat, and in that motion might\nOmit a ward or forfeit an offence\nWhich craved that very time. It is much better\nI am not there.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Cornets. A great cry and noise within crying “À Palamon!”]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh better never born\nThan minister to such harm.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Servant.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is the chance?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The cry’s “À Palamon.”","speaker":"SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then he has won. ’Twas ever likely.\nHe looked all grace and success, and he is\nDoubtless the prim’st of men. I prithee run\nAnd tell me how it goes.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Shout and cornets, crying “À Palamon!”]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Still “Palamon.”","speaker":"SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Run and enquire.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exit Servant.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor servant, thou hast lost.\nUpon my right side still I wore thy picture,\nPalamon’s on the left. Why so, I know not.\nI had no end in ’t else; chance would have it so.\nOn the sinister side the heart lies; Palamon\nHad the best-boding chance.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Another cry and shout within, and cornets.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This burst of clamour\nIs sure th’ end o’ th’ combat.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Servant.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They said that Palamon had Arcite’s body\nWithin an inch o’ th’ pyramid, that the cry\nWas general “À Palamon.” But anon,\nTh’ assistants made a brave redemption, and\nThe two bold titlers at this instant are\nHand to hand at it.","speaker":"SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Were they metamorphosed\nBoth into one—O, why? There were no woman\nWorth so composed a man! Their single share,\nTheir nobleness peculiar to them, gives\nThe prejudice of disparity, value’s shortness,\nTo any lady breathing.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Cornets. Cry within, “Arcite, Arcite.”]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"More exulting?\n“Palamon” still?","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, now the sound is “Arcite.”","speaker":"SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I prithee, lay attention to the cry;\nSet both thine ears to th’ business.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Cornets. A great shout and cry “Arcite, victory!”]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The cry is\n“Arcite”, and “Victory!” Hark, “Arcite, victory!”\nThe combat’s consummation is proclaimed\nBy the wind instruments.","speaker":"SERVANT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Half-sights saw\nThat Arcite was no babe. God’s lid, his richness\nAnd costliness of spirit looked through him; it could\nNo more be hid in him than fire in flax,\nThan humble banks can go to law with waters\nThat drift-winds force to raging. I did think\nGood Palamon would miscarry, yet I knew not\nWhy I did think so. Our reasons are not prophets\nWhen oft our fancies are. They are coming off.\nAlas, poor Palamon!","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Cornets. Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Pirithous, Arcite\nas victor, and Attendants.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lo, where our sister is in expectation,\nYet quaking and unsettled.—Fairest Emily,\nThe gods by their divine arbitrament\nHave given you this knight; he is a good one\nAs ever struck at head. Give me your hands.\nReceive you her, you him; be plighted with\nA love that grows as you decay.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Emily,\nTo buy you, I have lost what’s dearest to me,\nSave what is bought; and yet I purchase cheaply,\nAs I do rate your value.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O loved sister,\nHe speaks now of as brave a knight as e’er\nDid spur a noble steed. Surely the gods\nWould have him die a bachelor, lest his race\nShould show i’ th’ world too godlike. His behaviour\nSo charmed me that methought Alcides was\nTo him a sow of lead. If I could praise\nEach part of him to th’ all I have spoke, your Arcite\nDid not lose by ’t, for he that was thus good\nEncountered yet his better. I have heard\nTwo emulous Philomels beat the ear o’ th’ night\nWith their contentious throats, now one the higher,\nAnon the other, then again the first,\nAnd by-and-by out-breasted, that the sense\nCould not be judge between ’em. So it fared\nGood space between these kinsmen, till heavens did\nMake hardly one the winner.—Wear the garland\nWith joy that you have won.—For the subdued,\nGive them our present justice, since I know\nTheir lives but pinch ’em. Let it here be done.\nThe scene’s not for our seeing. Go we hence\nRight joyful, with some sorrow.—Arm your prize;\nI know you will not lose her.—Hippolyta,\nI see one eye of yours conceives a tear,\nThe which it will deliver.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is this winning?\nO all you heavenly powers, where is your mercy?\nBut that your wills have said it must be so,\nAnd charge me live to comfort this unfriended,\nThis miserable prince, that cuts away\nA life more worthy from him than all women,\nI should and would die too.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Infinite pity\nThat four such eyes should be so fixed on one\nThat two must needs be blind for ’t.","speaker":"HIPPOLYTA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So it is.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.3:51"}],"scene":3,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"5.4","label":"Act 5 · SCENE IV. The same; a Block prepared","act":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Palamon and his Knights pinioned;\nJailer, Executioner and Guard.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There’s many a man alive that hath outlived\nThe love o’ th’ people; yea, i’ th’ selfsame state\nStands many a father with his child. Some comfort\nWe have by so considering. We expire,\nAnd not without men’s pity; to live still,\nHave their good wishes; we prevent\nThe loathsome misery of age, beguile\nThe gout and rheum that in lag hours attend\nFor gray approachers; we come towards the gods\nYoung and unwappered, not halting under crimes\nMany and stale. That sure shall please the gods\nSooner than such, to give us nectar with ’em,\nFor we are more clear spirits. My dear kinsmen,\nWhose lives for this poor comfort are laid down,\nYou have sold ’em too too cheap.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What ending could be\nOf more content? O’er us the victors have\nFortune, whose title is as momentary,\nAs to us death is certain. A grain of honour\nThey not o’erweigh us.","speaker":"FIRST KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us bid farewell;\nAnd with our patience anger tottering Fortune,\nWho at her certain’st reels.","speaker":"SECOND KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come; who begins?","speaker":"THIRD KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E’en he that led you to this banquet shall\nTaste to you all.—Ah ha, my friend, my friend,\nYour gentle daughter gave me freedom once;\nYou’ll see ’t done now for ever. Pray, how does she?\nI heard she was not well; her kind of ill\nGave me some sorrow.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, she’s well restored,\nAnd to be married shortly.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my short life,\nI am most glad on’t. ’Tis the latest thing\nI shall be glad of; prithee, tell her so.\nCommend me to her, and, to piece her portion,\nTender her this.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Gives him his purse.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay let’s be offerers all.","speaker":"FIRST KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it a maid?","speaker":"SECOND KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Verily, I think so.\nA right good creature, more to me deserving\nThen I can ’quite or speak of.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Commend us to her.","speaker":"ALL KNIGHTS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[They give their purses.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods requite you all, and make her thankful.","speaker":"JAILER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Adieu; and let my life be now as short\nAs my leave-taking.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Lays his head on the block.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lead, courageous cousin.","speaker":"FIRST KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We’ll follow cheerfully.","speaker":"SECOND AND THIRD KNIGHT","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[A great noise within crying “Run!” “Save!” “Hold!”]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter in haste a Messenger.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold, hold! O hold, hold, hold!","speaker":"MESSENGER","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Pirithous in haste.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold, ho! It is a cursed haste you made\nIf you have done so quickly!—Noble Palamon,\nThe gods will show their glory in a life\nThat thou art yet to lead.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Can that be,\nWhen Venus, I have said, is false? How do things fare?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arise, great sir, and give the tidings ear\nThat are most dearly sweet and bitter.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What\nHath waked us from our dream?","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"List, then. Your cousin,\nMounted upon a steed that Emily\nDid first bestow on him, a black one, owing\nNot a hair-worth of white, which some will say\nWeakens his price, and many will not buy\nHis goodness with this note, which superstition\nHere finds allowance—on this horse is Arcite\nTrotting the stones of Athens, which the calkins\nDid rather tell than trample; for the horse\nWould make his length a mile, if ’t pleased his rider\nTo put pride in him. As he thus went counting\nThe flinty pavement, dancing, as ’twere, to th’ music\nHis own hooves made—for, as they say, from iron\nCame music’s origin—what envious flint,\nCold as old Saturn, and like him possessed\nWith fire malevolent, darted a spark,\nOr what fierce sulphur else, to this end made,\nI comment not; the hot horse, hot as fire,\nTook toy at this and fell to what disorder\nHis power could give his will; bounds, comes on end,\nForgets school-doing, being therein trained\nAnd of kind manage. Pig-like he whines\nAt the sharp rowel, which he frets at rather\nThan any jot obeys; seeks all foul means\nOf boist’rous and rough jad’ry to disseat\nHis lord that kept it bravely. When naught served,\nWhen neither curb would crack, girth break, nor diff’ring plunges\nDisroot his rider whence he grew, but that\nHe kept him ’tween his legs, on his hind hoofs\nOn end he stands\nThat Arcite’s legs, being higher than his head,\nSeemed with strange art to hang. His victor’s wreath\nEven then fell off his head and presently\nBackward the jade comes o’er, and his full poise\nBecomes the rider’s load. Yet is he living,\nBut such a vessel ’tis that floats but for\nThe surge that next approaches. He much desires\nTo have some speech with you. Lo, he appears.","speaker":"PIRITHOUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Theseus, Hippolyta, Emilia, Arcite in a chair.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O miserable end of our alliance!\nThe gods are mighty. Arcite, if thy heart,\nThy worthy, manly heart, be yet unbroken,\nGive me thy last words. I am Palamon,\nOne that yet loves thee dying.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take Emilia\nAnd with her all the world’s joy. Reach thy hand;\nFarewell. I have told my last hour. I was false,\nYet never treacherous. Forgive me, cousin.\nOne kiss from fair Emilia.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Emilia kisses\nArcite.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"’Tis done.\nTake her. I die.","speaker":"ARCITE","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy brave soul seek Elysium!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Arcite dies.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I’ll close thine eyes, Prince; blessed souls be with thee!\nThou art a right good man, and, while I live,\nThis day I give to tears.","speaker":"EMILIA","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I to honour.","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In this place first you fought; e’en very here\nI sundered you. Acknowledge to the gods\nOur thanks that you are living.\nHis part is played, and, though it were too short,\nHe did it well; your day is lengthened, and\nThe blissful dew of heaven does arrose you.\nThe powerful Venus well hath graced her altar,\nAnd given you your love. Our master Mars,\nHath vouched his oracle, and to Arcite gave\nThe grace of the contention. So the deities\nHave showed due justice.—Bear this hence.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O cousin,\nThat we should things desire, which do cost us\nThe loss of our desire! That naught could buy\nDear love, but loss of dear love!","speaker":"PALAMON","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Arcite’s body is carried out.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never Fortune\nDid play a subtler game. The conquered triumphs;\nThe victor has the loss; yet in the passage\nThe gods have been most equal. Palamon,\nYour kinsman hath confessed the right o’ th’ lady\nDid lie in you, for you first saw her and\nEven then proclaimed your fancy. He restored her\nAs your stol’n jewel and desired your spirit\nTo send him hence forgiven. The gods my justice\nTake from my hand and they themselves become\nThe executioners. Lead your lady off\nAnd call your lovers from the stage of death,\nWhom I adopt my friends. A day or two\nLet us look sadly, and give grace unto\nThe funeral of Arcite, in whose end\nThe visages of bridegrooms we’ll put on\nAnd smile with Palamon; for whom an hour,\nBut one hour since, I was as dearly sorry\nAs glad of Arcite, and am now as glad\nAs for him sorry. O you heavenly charmers,\nWhat things you make of us! For what we lack\nWe laugh, for what we have are sorry, still\nAre children in some kind. Let us be thankful\nFor that which is, and with you leave dispute\nThat are above our question. Let’s go off\nAnd bear us like the time.","speaker":"THESEUS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exeunt.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:5.4:42"}],"scene":4,"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"},{"id":"epilogue","label":"EPILOGUE","act":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Epilogue.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:epilogue:1"},{"kind":"text","text":"EPILOGUE\nI would now ask ye how you like the play,\nBut, as it is with schoolboys, cannot say.\nI am cruel fearful! Pray yet, stay a while,\nAnd let me look upon ye. No man smile?\nThen it goes hard, I see. He that has\nLoved a young handsome wench, then, show his face—\n’Tis strange if none be here—and, if he will,\nAgainst his conscience let him hiss, and kill\nOur market. ’Tis in vain, I see, to stay ye.\nHave at the worst can come, then! Now what say ye?\nAnd yet mistake me not: I am not bold;\nWe have no such cause. If the tale we have told\nFor ’tis no other—any way content ye—\nFor to that honest purpose it was meant ye—\nWe have our end; and you shall have ere long,\nI dare say, many a better, to prolong\nYour old loves to us. We, and all our might,\nRest at your service. Gentlemen, good night.","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:epilogue:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"[Flourish. Exit.]","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:epilogue:3"},{"kind":"heading","text":"FINIS","id":"two_noble_kinsmen:epilogue:4"}],"sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1506"}],"sourceLabel":"Project Gutenberg","sectionCount":27,"blockCount":1061,"wordCount":24515};
